window.QUESTION_BANK = [{"id":1,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has developed a model and computed the RMSE of the model on the test set. They have assigned this value to the variable rmse. They now want to manually store the RMSE value with the MLflow run. They write the following incomplete code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q1-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following lines of code can be used to fill in the blank so the code block can successfully complete the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"log_artifact"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"log_model"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"log_metric"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"log_param"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no way to store values like this."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"RMSE는 숫자 지표이므로 mlflow.log_metric으로 기록합니다. log_artifact는 파일용입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":2,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following MLflow operations can be used to automatically calculate and log a Shapley feature importance plot?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.shap.log_explanation"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these operations can accomplish the task."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.shap"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_figure"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.log_artifact"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"Shapley 설명을 계산하고 기록하는 API는 mlflow.shap.log_explanation입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":3,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has developed a scikit-learn random forest model, but they have not yet logged the model with MLflow. They want to obtain the input schema and the output schema of the model so they can document what type of data is expected as input. Which of the following MLflow operations can be used to perform this task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.models.schema.infer_schema"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.models.signature.infer_signature"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.models.Model.get_input_schema"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.models.Model.signature"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no way to obtain the input schema and the output schema of an unlogged model."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":4,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer and data scientist are working together to convert a batch deployment to an always-on streaming deployment. The machine learning engineer has expressed that rigorous data tests must be put in place as a part of their conversion to account for potential changes in data formats. Which of the following describes why these types of data type tests and checks are particularly important for streaming deployments?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Because the streaming deployment is always on, all types of data must be handled without producing an error"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"All of these statements"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Because the streaming deployment is always on, there is no practitioner to debug poor model performance"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Because the streaming deployment is always on, there is a need to confirm that the deployment can autoscale"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these statements"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":5,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which deployment paradigm can centrally compute predictions for a single record with exceedingly fast results?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Streaming"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Batch"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Edge/on-device"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these strategies will accomplish the task."}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Real-time"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":6,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineering team wants to build a continuous pipeline for data preparation of a machine learning application. The team would like the data to be fully processed and made ready for inference in a series of equal-sized batches. Which tool can be used to provide this type of continuous processing?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Spark UDFs"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Structured Streaming"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Delta Lake"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"연속적으로 같은 크기 마이크로배치를 만드는 도구는 Structured Streaming입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":7,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to deploy a model for real-time serving using MLflow Model Serving. For the model, the machine learning engineer currently has one model version in each of the stages in the MLflow Model Registry. The engineer wants to know which model versions can be queried once Model Serving is enabled for the model. Which of the following lists all of the MLflow Model Registry stages whose model versions are automatically deployed with Model Serving?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Staging, Production, Archived"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Production"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None, Staging, Production, Archived"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Staging, Production"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None, Staging, Production"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":8,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has written a function to track the runs of their random forest model. The data scientist is changing the number of trees in the forest across each run. Which of the following MLflow operations is designed to log single values like the number of trees in a random forest?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_artifact"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_model"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_metric"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_param"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no way to store values like this."}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"트리 개수처럼 실행마다 직접 정하는 값은 파라미터가므로 mlflow.log_param을 씁니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":9,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is converting a Hyperopt-based hyperparameter tuning process from manual MLflow logging to MLflow Autologging. They are trying to determine how to manage nested Hyperopt runs with MLflow Autologging. Which of the following approaches will create a single parent run for the process and a child run for each unique combination of hyperparameter values when using Hyperopt and MLflow Autologging?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Starting a manual parent run before calling fmin"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Ensuring that a built-in model flavor is used for the model logging"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Starting a manual child run within the objective function"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no way to accomplish nested runs with MLflow Autologging and Hyperopt"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Autologging will automatically accomplish this task with Hyperopt"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":10,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has created a Python function compute_features that returns a Spark DataFrame with the following schema:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q10-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"The resulting DataFrame is assigned to the features_df variable. The data scientist wants to create a Feature Store table using features_df. Which of the following code blocks can they use to create and populate the Feature Store table using the Feature Store Client fs?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q10-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q10-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"features_df.write.mode(\"fs\").path(\"new_table\")"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q10-4.jpg"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"features_df.write.mode(\"feature\").path(\"new_table\")"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":["D"],"note":"Feature Store 테이블을 생성하면서 features_df의 데이터까지 적재하려면 fs.create_table에 df=features_df를 전달합니다. function은 create_table의 매개변수가 아닙니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":11,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following is a benefit of logging a model signature with an MLflow model?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model will have a unique identifier in the MLflow experiment"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The schema of input data can be validated when serving models"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model can be deployed using real-time serving tools"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model will be secured by the user that developed it"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The schema of input data will be converted to match the signature"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":["E"],"note":"시그니처의 목적은 서빙 시 입력 스키마를 검증하는 것입니다. 입력을 자동 변환하지는 않습니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":12,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which statement describes streaming with Spark as a model deployment strategy?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The inference of batch processed records as soon as a trigger is hit"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The inference of all types of records in real-time"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The inference of batch processed records as soon as a Spark job is run"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The inference of incrementally processed records as soon as trigger is hit"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The inference of incrementally processed records as soon as a Spark job is run"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["E"],"note":"Spark 스트리밍 배포는 트리거가 발생할 때마다 증분 레코드에 대해 추론합니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":13,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has deployed a model recommender using MLflow Model Serving. They now want to query the version of that model that is in the Production stage of the MLflow Model Registry. Which of the following model URIs can be used to query the described model version?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"https://<databricks-instance>/model-serving/recommender/Production/invocations"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The version number of the model version in Production is necessary to complete this task."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"https://<databricks-instance>/model/recommender/stage-production/invocations"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"https://<databricks-instance>/model-serving/recommender/stage-production/invocations"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"https://<databricks-instance>/model/recommender/Production/invocations"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["B"],"note":"MLflow Model Serving에서는 버전 번호 없이 https://<databricks-instance>/model/<모델명>/Production/invocations 주소로 Production 모델을 호출할 수 있습니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":14,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following tools can assist in real-time deployments by packaging software with its own application, tools, and libraries?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Cloud-based compute"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these tools"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"REST APIs"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Containers"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Autoscaling clusters"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"애플리케이션과 라이브러리를 함께 패키징하는 도구는 컨테이너입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":15,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has registered a sklearn model in the MLflow Model Registry using the sklearn model flavor with a model_uri. Which operation can be used to load the model as an sklearn object for batch deployment?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.spark.load_model(model_uri)"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.pyfunc.read_model(model_uri)"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.read_model(model_uri)"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.pyfunc.load_model(model_uri)"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.load_model(model_uri)"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["D"],"note":"sklearn 객체로 다시 읽으려면 mlflow.sklearn.load_model을 사용합니다. pyfunc는 공통 래퍼입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":16,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist set up a machine learning pipeline to automatically log a data visualization with each run. They now want to view the visualizations in Databricks. Which location in Databricks will show these data visualizations?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The MLflow Model Registry Model page"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Artifacts section of the MLflow Experiment page"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Logged data visualizations cannot be viewed in Databricks"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Artifacts section of the MLflow Run page"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Figures section of the MLflow Run page"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["E"],"note":"로그한 그림은 해당 Run 페이지의 Artifacts에 있습니다. Figures라는 섹션은 없습니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":17,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has developed a scikit-learn model sklearn_model and they want to log the model using MLflow. They write the following incomplete code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q17-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which lines of code can be used to fill in the blank so the code block can successfully complete the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.spark.track_model(sklearn_model, \"model\")"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.log_model(sklearn_model, \"model\")"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.spark.log_model(sklearn_model, \"model\")"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.load_model(\"model\")"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.track_model(sklearn_model, \"model\")"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"scikit-learn 모델을 기록하는 API는 mlflow.sklearn.log_model입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":18,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following describes the concept of MLflow Model flavors?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"A convention that deployment tools can use to wrap preprocessing logic into a Model"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"A convention that MLflow Model Registry can use to version models"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"A convention that MLflow Experiments can use to organize their Runs by project"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"A convention that deployment tools can use to understand the model"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"A convention that MLflow Model Registry can use to organize its Models by project"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"Model flavor는 배포 도구가 모델을 이해하도록 하는 규약입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":19,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"In a continuous integration, continuous deployment (CI/CD) process for machine learning pipelines, which of the following events commonly triggers the execution of automated testing?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The launch of a new cost-efficient SQL endpoint"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"CI/CD pipelines are not needed for machine learning pipelines"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The arrival of a new feature table in the Feature Store"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The launch of a new cost-efficient job cluster"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The arrival of a new model version in the MLflow Model Registry"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["D"],"note":"ML 파이프라인 CI에서 자동 테스트를 흔히 깨우는 이벤트는 레지스트리에 새 모델 버전이 등록되는 일입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":20,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineering team has written predictions computed in a batch job to a Delta table for querying. However, the team has noticed that the querying is running slowly. The team has already tuned the size of the data files. Upon investigating, the team has concluded that the rows meeting the query condition are sparsely located throughout each of the data files. Based on the scenario, which optimization technique could speed up the query by colocating similar records while considering values in multiple columns?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Z-Ordering"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Bin-packing"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Write as a Parquet file"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Data skipping"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Tuning the file size"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":21,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer needs to deliver predictions of a machine learning model in real-time. However, the feature values needed for computing the predictions are available one week before the query time. Which feature is a benefit of using a batch serving deployment in this scenario rather than a real-time serving deployment where predictions are computed at query time?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Batch serving has built-in capabilities in Databricks Machine Learning"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no advantage to using batch serving deployments over real-time serving deployments"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Computing predictions in real-time provides more up-to-date results"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Testing is not possible in real-time serving deployments"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Querying stored predictions can be faster than computing predictions in real-time"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"피처가 미리 준비된 경우, 배치로 저장해 둔 예측을 조회하는 편이 요청 시점에 계산하는 것보다 빠릅니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":22,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has developed a random forest model using scikit-learn, logged the model using MLflow as random_forest_model, and stored its run ID in the run_id Python variable. They now want to deploy that model by performing batch inference on a Spark DataFrame spark_df. Which of the following code blocks can they use to create a function called predict that they can use to complete the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q22-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It is not possible to deploy a scikit-learn model on a Spark DataFrame."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q22-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q22-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q22-4.jpg"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["D"],"note":"기록된 MLflow 모델을 Spark DataFrame 배치 추론용 UDF로 불러오려면 mlflow.pyfunc.spark_udf(spark, f'runs:/{run_id}/random_forest_model')을 씁니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":23,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following describes the purpose of the context parameter in the predict method of Python models for MLflow?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The context parameter allows the user to specify which version of the registered MLflow Model should be used based on the given application's current scenario"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The context parameter allows the user to document the performance of a model after it has been deployed"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The context parameter allows the user to include relevant details of the business case to allow downstream users to understand the purpose of the model"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The context parameter allows the user to provide the model with completely custom if-else logic for the given application's current scenario"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The context parameter allows the user to provide the model access to objects like preprocessing models or custom configuration files"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"PythonModel.predict의 context는 전처리 모델이나 설정 파일 같은 아티팩트에 접근할 때 씁니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":24,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has developed a model and registered it using the FeatureStoreClient fs. The model has model URI model_uri. The engineer now needs to perform batch inference on customer-level Spark DataFrame spark_df, but it is missing a few of the static features that were used when training the model. The customer_id column is the primary key of spark_df and the training set used when training and logging the model. Which of the following code blocks can be used to compute predictions for spark_df when the missing feature values can be found in the Feature Store by searching for features by customer_id?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"df = fs.get_missing_features(spark_df, model_uri) fs.score_model(model_uri, df)"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.score_model(model_uri, spark_df)"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"df = fs.get_missing_features(spark_df, model_uri) fs.score_batch(model_uri, df)"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"df = fs.get_missing_features(spark_df) fs.score_batch(model_uri, df)"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.score_batch(model_uri, spark_df)"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":25,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer needs to select a deployment strategy for a new machine learning application. The feature values are not available until the time of delivery, and results are needed exceedingly fast for one record at a time. Which of the following deployment strategies can be used to meet these requirements?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Edge/on-device"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Streaming"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these strategies will meet the requirements."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Batch"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Real-time"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":26,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is using the following code block as part of a batch deployment pipeline:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q26-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following changes needs to be made so this code block will work when the inference table is a stream source?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace \"inference\" with the path to the location of the Delta table"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace schema(schema) with option(\"maxFilesPerTrigger\", 1}"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace spark.read with spark.readStream"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace format(\"delta\") with format(\"stream\")"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace predict with a stream-friendly prediction function"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":["B"],"note":"Delta 스트림 소스를 읽으려면 spark.read를 spark.readStream으로 바꿉니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":27,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is migrating a machine learning pipeline to use Databricks Machine Learning. They have programmatically identified the best run from an MLflow Experiment and stored its URI in the model_uri variable and its Run ID in the run_id variable. They have also determined that the model was logged with the name \"model\". Now, the machine learning engineer wants to register that model in the MLflow Model Registry with the name \"best_model\". Which line of code can they use to register the model to the MLflow Model Registry?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.register_model(model_uri, \"best_model\")"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.register_model(run_id, \"best_model\")"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.register_model(f\"runs:/{run_id}/best_model\", \"model\")"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.register_model(model_uri, \"model\")"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.register_model(f\"runs:/{run_id}/model\")"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":28,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to move their model version model_version for the MLflow Model Registry model `model` from the Staging stage to the Production stage using MLflow Client client. Which of the following code blocks can they use to accomplish the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q28-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q28-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q28-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q28-4.jpg"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q28-5.jpg"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"Production으로 전이하려면 client.transition_model_version_stage(name=model, version=model_version, stage='Production')을 호출합니다. 보기 B와 C의 그림이 같아 둘 다 정답으로 인정합니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":["B","C"]},{"id":29,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is manually refreshing a model in an existing machine learning pipeline. The pipeline uses the MLflow Model Registry model \"project\". The machine learning engineer would like to add a new version of the model to \"project\". Which MLflow operation can the machine learning engineer use to accomplish this task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.register_model"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MlflowClient.update_registered_model"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.add_model_version"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MlflowClient.get_model_version"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The machine learning engineer needs to create an entirely new MLflow Model Registry model"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":["B"],"note":"기존 등록 모델에 새 버전을 추가하는 연산은 mlflow.register_model입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":30,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following is an advantage of using the python_function(pyfunc) model flavor over the built-in library-specific model flavors?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"python_function provides no benefits over the built-in library-specific model flavors"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"python_function can be used to deploy models in a parallelizable fashion"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"python_function can be used to deploy models without worrying about which library was used to create the model"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"python_function can be used to store models in an MLmodel file"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"python_function can be used to deploy models without worrying about whether they are deployed in batch, streaming, or real-time environments"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":["B"],"note":"pyfunc의 장점은 원래 학습 라이브러리에 묶이지 않고 같은 방식으로 배포할 수 있다는 점입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":31,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following lists all of the model stages are available in the MLflow Model Registry?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Development. Staging. Production"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None. Staging. Production"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Staging. Production. Archived"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None. Staging. Production. Archived"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Development. Staging. Production. Archived"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":32,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following MLflow operations can be used to delete a model from the MLflow Model Registry?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.transition_model_version_stage"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.delete_model_version"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.update_registered_model"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.delete_model"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.delete_registered_model"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":33,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to programmatically create a new Databricks Job whose schedule depends on the result of some automated tests in a machine learning pipeline. Which Databricks tool can be used to programmatically create the Job?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow APIs"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"AutoML APIs"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Client"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Jobs cannot be created programmatically"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Databricks REST APIs"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":34,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to view all of the active MLflow Model Registry Webhooks for a specific model. They are using the following code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q34-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following changes does the machine learning engineer need to make to this code block so it will successfully accomplish the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There are no necessary changes"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace list with view in the endpoint URL"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace POST with GET in the call to http request"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace list with webhooks in the endpoint URL"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace POST with PUT in the call to http request"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":35,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has created a webhook with the following code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q35-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following code blocks will trigger this webhook to run the associate job?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q35-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q35-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q35-4.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q35-5.jpg"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q35-6.jpg"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"MODEL_VERSION_TRANSITIONED_TO_STAGING 웹훅을 깨우려면 client.transition_model_version_stage(name=model, version=model_version, stage='Staging')을 호출합니다. 보기 A와 E의 그림이 같아 둘 다 정답으로 인정합니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":["A","E"]},{"id":36,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following describes concept drift?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Concept drift is when there is a change in the distribution of an input variable"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Concept drift is when there is a change in the distribution of a target variable"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Concept drift is when there is a change in the relationship between input variables and target variables"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Concept drift is when there is a change in the distribution of the predicted target given by the model"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these describe Concept drift"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":["D"],"note":"컨셉 드리프트는 입력과 타깃의 관계가 바뀔 때입니다. 예측값 분포 변화는 prediction drift입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":37,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is monitoring categorical input variables for a production machine learning application. The engineer believes that missing values are becoming more prevalent in more recent data for a particular value in one of the categorical input variables. Which of the following tools can the machine learning engineer use to assess their theory?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) test"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"One-way Chi-squared Test"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Two-way Chi-squared Test"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Jensen-Shannon distance"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":38,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist is using MLflow to track their machine learning experiment. As a part of each MLflow run, they are performing hyperparameter tuning. The data scientist would like to have one parent run for the tuning process with a child run for each unique combination of hyperparameter values. They are using the following code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q38-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"The code block is not nesting the runs in MLflow as they expected. Which of the following changes does the data scientist need to make to the above code block so that it successfully nests the child runs under the parent run in MLflow?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Indent the child run blocks within the parent run block"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Add the nested=True argument to the parent run"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Remove the nested=True argument from the child runs"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Provide the same name to the run name parameter for all three run blocks"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Add the nested=True argument to the parent run and remove the nested=True arguments from the child runs"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":["E"],"note":"자식 실행을 부모 실행 아래에 두려면 자식 with mlflow.start_run(..., nested=True): 블록을 부모 with 블록 안으로 들여씁니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":39,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to log feature importance data from a CSV file at path importance_path with an MLflow run for model. Which code block will accomplish this task inside of an existing MLflow run block?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q39-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q39-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_data(importance_path, \"feature-importance.csv\")"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_artifact(importance_path, \"feature-importance.csv\")"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these code blocks can accomplish the task."}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"실행 블록 안에서 CSV 파일을 아티팩트로 기록하는 함수는 mlflow.log_artifact(importance_path, 'feature-importance.csv')입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":40,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following is a simple, low-cost method of monitoring numeric feature drift?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Jensen-Shannon test"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Summary statistics trends"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Chi-squared test"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these can be used to monitor feature drift"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) test"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":41,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has developed a model to predict ice cream sales using the expected temperature and expected number of hours of sun in the day. However, the expected temperature is dropping beneath the range of the input variable on which the model was trained. Which type of drift is present in the above scenario?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Label drift"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Concept drift"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Prediction drift"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Feature drift"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":42,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist wants to remove the star_rating column from the Delta table at the location path. To do this, they need to load in data and drop the star_rating column. Which of the following code blocks accomplishes this task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.format(\"delta\").load(path).drop(\"star_rating\")"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.format(\"delta\").table(path).drop(\"star_rating\")"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Delta tables cannot be modified"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.table(path).drop(\"star_rating\")"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.sql(\"SELECT * EXCEPT star_rating FROM path\")"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":["D"],"note":"경로에 있는 Delta 테이블은 spark.read.format('delta').load(path)로 읽습니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":43,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following operations in Feature Store Client fs can be used to return a Spark DataFrame of a data set associated with a Feature Store table?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.create_table"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.write_table"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.get_table"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no way to accomplish this task with fs"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.read_table"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"Feature Store 테이블을 Spark DataFrame으로 읽는 메서드는 fs.read_table입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":44,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is in the process of implementing a concept drift monitoring solution. They are planning to use the following steps: 1. Deploy a model to production and compute predicted values 2. Obtain the observed (actual) label values 3. _____ 4. Run a statistical test to determine if there are changes over time Which of the following should be completed as Step #3?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Obtain the observed values (actual) feature values"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Measure the latency of the prediction time"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Retrain the model"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these should be completed as Step #3"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Compute the evaluation metric using the observed and predicted values"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["D"],"note":"컨셉 드리프트 감시는 예측값과 실제 레이블을 모은 뒤(1·2단계), 두 값으로 평가 지표를 계산하고(3단계), 시간에 따른 변화를 검정합니다(4단계).","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":45,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which statement is a reason for using Jensen-Shannon (JS) distance over a Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) test for numeric feature drift detection?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"All of these reasons"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"JS is not normalized or smoothed"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these reasons"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"JS is more robust when working with large datasets"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"JS does not require any manual threshold or cutoff determinations"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":46,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist is utilizing MLflow to track their machine learning experiments. After completing a series of runs for the experiment with experiment ID exp_id, the data scientist wants to programmatically work with the experiment run data in a Spark DataFrame. They have an active MLflow Client client and an active Spark session spark. Which of the following lines of code can be used to obtain run-level results for exp_id in a Spark DataFrame?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.list_run_infos(exp_id)"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.format(\"delta\").load(exp_id)"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no way to programmatically return row-level results from an MLflow Experiment."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.search_runs(exp_id)"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.format(\"mlflow-experiment\").load(exp_id)"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["B"],"note":"Databricks에서 실험(exp_id)의 실행 데이터를 Spark DataFrame으로 읽으려면 spark.read.format('mlflow-experiment').load(exp_id)를 사용합니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":47,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following MLflow Model Registry use cases requires the use of an HTTP Webhook?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Starting a testing job when a new model is registered"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Updating data in a source table for a Databricks SQL dashboard when a model version transitions to the Production stage"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Sending an email alert when an automated testing Job fails"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these use cases require the use of an HTTP Webhook"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Sending a message to a Slack channel when a model version transitions stages"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["B"],"note":"스테이지 전이 시 Slack 채널 같은 외부 주소로 알림을 보내려면 HTTP Webhook이 필요합니다. Databricks 작업 실행은 Job Webhook으로 처리합니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":48,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to log and deploy a model as an MLflow pyfunc model. They have custom preprocessing that needs to be completed on feature variables prior to fitting the model or computing predictions using that model. They decide to wrap this preprocessing in a custom model class ModelWithPreprocess, where the preprocessing is performed when calling fit and when calling predict. They then log the fitted model of the ModelWithPreprocess class as a pyfunc model. Which statement is a benefit of this approach when loading the logged pyfunc model for downstream deployment?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The pyfunc model can be used to deploy models in a parallelizable fashion"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The same preprocessing logic will automatically be applied when calling fit"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The same preprocessing logic will automatically be applied when calling predict"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"This approach has no impact when loading the logged Pyfunc model for downstream deployment"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no longer a need for pipeline-like machine learning objects"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":["E"],"note":"전처리를 predict 안에 넣으면, 로드한 pyfunc를 호출할 때 같은 전처리가 다시 적용됩니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":49,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineering manager has asked all of the engineers on their team to add text descriptions to each of the model projects in the MLflow Model Registry. They are starting with the model project \"model\" and they'd like to add the text in the model_description variable. The team is using the following line of code:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q49-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which change does the team need to make to the above code block to accomplish the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace update_registered_model with update_model_version"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There no changes necessary"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace description with artifact"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace client.update_registered_model with mlflow"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Add a Python model as an argument to update_registered_model"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":50,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to move their model version model_version for the MLflow Model Registry model `model` from the Staging stage to the Production stage using MLflow Client client. At the same time, they would like to archive any model versions that are already in the Production stage. Which code block can they use to accomplish the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q50-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q50-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q50-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q50-4.jpg"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"기존 Production 버전을 아카이브하며 전이하려면 client.transition_model_version_stage(..., stage='Production', archive_existing_versions=True)를 사용합니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":51,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following Databricks-managed MLflow capabilities is a centralized model store?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Models"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model Registry"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model Serving"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Feature Store"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Experiments"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":52,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is attempting to create a webhook that will trigger a Databricks Job job_id when a model version for model transitions into any MLflow Model Registry stage. They have the following incomplete code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q52-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which lines of code can be used to fill in the blank so that the code block accomplishes the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"\"MODEL_VERSION_CREATED\""}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"\"MODEL_VERSION_TRANSITIONED_TO_PRODUCTION\""}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"\"MODEL_VERSION_TRANSITIONED_TO_STAGING\""}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"\"MODEL_VERSION_TRANSITIONED_STAGE\""}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"\"MODEL_VERSION_TRANSITIONED_TO_STAGING\", \"MODEL_VERSION_TRANSITIONED_TO_PRODUCTION\""}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"모든 스테이지 전이를 받으려면 MODEL_VERSION_TRANSITIONED_STAGE 이벤트를 사용합니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":53,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has developed and logged a Spark ML random forest model, and then they ended their Spark session and terminated their cluster. After starting a new cluster, they want to review the featureImportances of the original model object. Which lines of code can be used to restore the model object so that featureImportances is available?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.load_model(model_uri)"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.list_artifacts(run_id)[\"feature-importances.csv\"]"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.load_model(model_uri)"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"This can only be viewed in the MLflow Experiments UI"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.pyfunc.load_model(model_uri)"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":54,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following is a simple statistic to monitor for categorical feature drift?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Mode"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Mode, number of unique values, and percentage of missing values"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Percentage of missing values"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Number of unique values"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":55,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following is a probable response to identifying drift in a machine learning application?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these responses"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Retraining and deploying a model on more recent data"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"All of these responses"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Rebuilding the machine learning application with a new label variable"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Sunsetting the machine learning application"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"드리프트에 대한 대응으로는 재학습, 레이블을 바꾼 재구성, 서비스 종료가 모두 가능합니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":56,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has computed updated feature values for all primary key values stored in the Feature Store table features. In addition, feature values for some new primary key values have also been computed. The updated feature values are stored in the DataFrame features_df. They want to replace all data in features with the newly computed data. Which of the following code blocks can they use to perform this task using the Feature Store Client fs?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q56-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q56-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q56-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q56-4.jpg"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q56-5.jpg"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["E"],"note":"기존 Feature Store 테이블의 데이터를 새 DataFrame으로 모두 덮어쓰려면 fs.write_table(name='features', df=features_df, mode='overwrite')를 사용합니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":57,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"After a data scientist noticed that a column was missing from a production feature set stored as a Delta table, the machine learning engineering team has been tasked with determining when the column was dropped from the feature set. Which SQL command can be used to accomplish this task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"VERSION"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"DESCRIBE"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"HISTORY"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"DESCRIBE HISTORY"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"TIMESTAMP"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":58,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following describes label drift?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Label drift is when there is a change in the distribution of the predicted target given by the model"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these describe label drift"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Label drift is when there is a change in the distribution of an input variable"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Label drift is when there is a change in the relationship between input variables and target variables"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Label drift is when there is a change in the distribution of a target variable"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"레이블 드리프트는 타깃 변수의 분포가 변하는 것입니다. 입력 분포 변화는 피처 드리프트입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":59,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following machine learning model deployment paradigms is the most common for machine learning projects?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"On-device"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Streaming"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Real-time"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Batch"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these deployments"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["B"],"note":"운영 ML 프로젝트에서 가장 흔한 배포 방식은 배치입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":60,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist would like to enable MLflow Autologging for all machine learning libraries used in a notebook. They want to ensure that MLflow Autologging is used no matter what version of the Databricks Runtime for Machine Learning is used to run the notebook and no matter what workspace-wide configurations are selected in the Admin Console. Which of the following lines of code can they use to accomplish this task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.autolog()"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.spark.autolog()"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.conf.set(\"autologging\", True)"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It is not possible to automatically log MLflow runs."}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.autolog()"}]}],"answer":["E"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"라이브러리에 상관없이 오토로깅을 켜는 호출은 mlflow.autolog()입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":61,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following describes batch deployment for machine learning projects?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Predictions are computed and delivered as soon as feature values are available"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these describe batch deployment for machine learning projects"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Predictions are computed prior to delivery and stored for later querying"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Predictions are computed immediately as data arrives and stored for later querying"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"In batch deployment, predictions are precomputed at scheduled intervals and stored for later querying. This approach is ideal when real-time inference is not required and when predictions can be generated in bulk ahead of time.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":62,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer needs to select a deployment strategy for a new machine learning application. The machine learning application requires central prediction computation and exceedingly fast results, but only a handful of predictions need to be computed at a time. Which deployment strategy can be used to meet these requirements?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Streaming"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Real-time"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Batch"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Edge/on-device"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Real-time deployment is the appropriate strategy when predictions need to be computed centrally with very low latency, even if only a small number of predictions are required at a time. This ensures fast responses for applications requiring immediate inference.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":63,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is in the process of implementing a feature drift monitoring solution. They are planning to use the following steps: 1. Measure the distributions of each feature variable in the training set 2. Deploy a model to production 3. Measure the distributions of each feature variable in inference 4. _______ Which action should be completed as Step #4?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Retrain the model based on any new feature variables that have been added to the feature set"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Obtain the observed values (actual) feature values and compare to the predicted values"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Measure the latency of the model's prediction time"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Run a statistical test to determine if there are changes in the feature variable distribution over time"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The final step in a feature drift monitoring solution is to run a statistical test (e.g., Kolmogorov-Smirnov test) to determine whether the feature distributions in production have significantly diverged from those in the training set. This helps detect drift and maintain model reliability.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":64,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has found drift in a production machine learning application. The engineer has determined that retraining and deploying a new model is necessary. Which statement must be true prior to deploying the new model?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"All of these statements must be true prior to deploying the new model"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The new model must perform better than the original model on a random subset of the available data"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The now model must perform better than the original model on all of the available data"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The new model must perform better than the original model on the most recently available data"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"When drift is detected, it indicates that the data distribution has changed. Therefore, the new model must perform better than the original model on the most recently available data, as that data reflects current conditions. This ensures the new model is well-adapted to the current environment and will deliver more reliable predictions.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":65,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Label drift occurs where there is a change in which element?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The relationship between input variables and target variables"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The distribution of the predicted target given by the model"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The distribution of a target variable"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The distribution of an input variable"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Label drift refers to a change in the distribution of the target variable over time. This means the frequencies or proportions of classes or target values shift, which can impact model performance even if the input feature distributions remain unchanged.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":66,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has detected that concept drift is occurring in a production machine learning application. Which result is the impact of concept drift?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model's latency will decrease"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model's efficacy will increase"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model's latency will increase"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model's efficacy will decrease"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Concept drift occurs when the relationship between input features and the target variable changes over time. This leads to a decrease in the model's efficacy, as the model is no longer accurately capturing the underlying data patterns it was trained on.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":67,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is monitoring label values for a production machine learning classification model. The engineer believes that the relative prevalence of the classes is changing in more recent data. Which tool can the machine learning engineer use to assess their theory?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"One-way Chi-squared Test"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Jensen-Shannon distance"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Two-way Chi-squared Test"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) test"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A One-way Chi-squared Test is used to compare observed class distributions with expected distributions to determine if there has been a significant shift. This makes it suitable for detecting label drift in classification tasks, where the relative prevalence of classes may be changing over time.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":68,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has run the following line of code to create a Feature Store table:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q68-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which statement correctly describes the data in the Feature Store table new_table?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The data in new_table is the values in column customer_id from df"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The data in new_table is exactly the same as the data in df"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The data in new_table is the customer-related features from df"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no data in new_table"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The fs.create_table() command defines metadata for a Feature Store table, such as its name, primary key, schema, and description - but it does not insert any data into the table. This command simply registers an empty table structure based on the provided schema (df.schema). To populate the table with data, a separate fs.write_table() or equivalent write operation must be executed afterward.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":69,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which MLflow operation can be used to log a plot that was generated during an MLflow run?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these operations can accomplish the task."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_metric"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_visualization"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_figure"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The mlflow.log_figure() operation is used to log plots or figures generated during an MLflow run. It takes a Matplotlib, Plotly, or similar figure object and saves it as an artifact in the MLflow tracking server, enabling visualization and later retrieval from the MLflow UI.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":70,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist would like to switch from manually using MLflow logging to MLflow Autologging for all machine learning libraries used in a notebook. They begin by adding mlflow.autolog()to the top of the below code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q70-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"The data scientist is now trying to determine which line of code will kick off the MLflow Autologging process. Which line of code within the above code block will start the MLflow Autologging process?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_metric(\"training_mse\", mse)"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.log_model(rf_model, \"rf_model\")"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"with mlflow.start_run(run_name=\"ML Project\") as run:"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"rf_model = rf.fit(X_train, y_train)"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The MLflow Autologging process is triggered when a model's training function is called. In this case, rf.fit(X_train, y_train) starts the autologging process because mlflow.autolog() automatically tracks parameters, metrics, and the model when a supported estimator (like RandomForestRegressor) is fitted. The context manager mlflow.start_run() only initiates an MLflow run, but the actual autologging begins during the .fit() execution.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":71,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has developed a scikit-learn random forest model, but they have not yet logged the model with MLflow. They have created a model signature model_signature and a few example input records input_records. Which block of code can be used to log all of this information?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q71-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.log_model(model, \"model\") mlflow.log_signature(model_signature) mlflow.log_input(input_records)"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There is no way to log a model signature."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q71-2.jpg"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The code block correctly uses the mlflow.sklearn.log_model() function with both signature and input_example parameters. signature=model_signature defines the expected model input and output schema, ensuring reproducibility and validation during inference. input_example=input_records provides a sample input that helps users understand the model's expected input format.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":72,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to load the data from the very first version of a Delta table from location path. Which of the following lines of code can be used to accomplish this task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.format(\"delta\").option(\"version\", 0).load(path)"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.format(\"delta\").option(\"versionAsOf\", 0).load(path)"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.format(\"delta\").option(\"versionAsOf\", 1).load(path)"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.format(\"delta\").option(\"version\", 1).load(path)"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"Delta 버전은 0부터 시작합니다. 최초 버전은 option('versionAsOf', 0)입니다.","explanation":"In Delta Lake, the option(\"versionAsOf\", <version_number>) parameter is used to load a specific version of a Delta table. Since Delta table versions are 1-indexed (the first version is version 1), using .option(\"versionAsOf\", 1) correctly loads the very first version of the table.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":73,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which is a benefit of logging an input example with an MLflow model?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model evaluation metrics are stored with the model"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The environment requirements are packaged with the MLmodel file"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"An example record is available to test serving applications"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"An example prediction is available to test post-inference pipelines"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Logging an input example with an MLflow model provides a concrete sample of the data format expected by the model. This helps serving applications and users understand the model's input structure and verify that inference requests are properly formatted when deploying or testing the model in production.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":74,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist set up a machine learning pipeline to automatically log feature importance data from a CSV with each run. They now want to view the feature importance data in Databricks. Which location in Databricks will show this data?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The MLflow Model Registry Model page"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Artifacts section of the MLflow Experiment page"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Artifacts section of the MLflow Run page"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Data section of the MLflow Run page"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"When a data scientist logs feature importance data (e.g., as a CSV or JSON file) using MLflow, it is stored as an artifact of that specific run. These artifacts are visible under the Artifacts section of the MLflow Run page in Databricks, where users can view or download the logged files associated with that particular experiment run.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":75,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist wants to log outlier feature data from a CSV file at path outlier_path with an MLflow run for model. Which code block will accomplish this task inside of an existing MLflow run block?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_artifact(outlier_path, \"outlier-features.csv\")"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_data(outlier_path, \"outlier-features.csv\")"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q75-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q75-2.jpg"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"To log external files, such as a CSV containing outlier feature data, MLflow provides the mlflow.log_artifact() function. This function uploads the specified file or directory (outlier_path) as an artifact under the provided artifact path (\"outlier-features.csv\"). It is the correct way to associate data files with an MLflow run, whereas mlflow.log_model() is reserved for logging model objects, not arbitrary data.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":76,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is responsible for maintaining a fraud detection model deployed on Databricks. They want to implement a retraining pipeline that automatically starts when the model's F1 score drops below a threshold or when input feature distributions change significantly. Which two actions should the engineer take to implement this automated retraining? (Choose two.)"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Schedule a recurring query on the Lakehouse monitoring table."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use MLflow to manually log metrics and retrain the model offline."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Set up a manual retraining schedule to run every week regardless of alerts."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Configure these alerts to send webhook notifications that trigger the model training job."}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use Databricks SQL to create alerts on model performance and data drift metrics stored in Delta tables."}]}],"answer":["D","E"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Databricks Lakehouse Monitoring stores model performance and data drift metrics in Delta tables, which can be monitored using Databricks SQL alerts. By creating alerts on F1 score degradation or significant feature drift and configuring those alerts to send webhook notifications, the engineer can automatically trigger a retraining job whenever predefined conditions are met, enabling event-driven, automated retraining aligned with MLOps best practices.","multi":true,"equiv":[]},{"id":77,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is using Spark ML to train a model for detecting fraudulent transactions (1: fraudulent, 0: non-fraudulent). To maximize the model's overall AUC (ROC) score, the Data Scientist wants to perform hyperparameter tuning on a Random Forest classifier. Due to budget constraints, the tuning process must be completed efficiently to avoid excessive compute costs. Which approach will fulfill their needs?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q77-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q77-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q77-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q77-4.jpg"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"TrainValidationSplit evaluates each hyperparameter combination using a single train/validation split, which is significantly more compute-efficient than k-fold cross-validation. Using a BinaryClassificationEvaluator aligns directly with optimizing AUC (ROC) for a binary fraud detection task, making this approach well suited for maximizing model performance under budget and compute constraints.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":78,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer needs to build a time series model. In Databricks, they have created isolated environments in different workspaces for development, staging, and production. To manage this model, they are planning on using a \"deploy code\" strategy. They are concerned that the model trained in development will not remain consistent across environments, due to differences in the dependencies installed. What can they do to ensure that the model is trained with the same packages?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the same Databricks Runtime (DBR) version across environments and define dependencies in a lock file that is installed as part of the job."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use custom init scripts that install the necessary packages as part of the job."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use MLflow to track the model parameters and metrics in development, then export the same training conditions in staging and production."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use Databricks Repos to store the model code and install the required libraries in the notebook."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Using the same Databricks Runtime across environments ensures a consistent base environment, and installing dependencies from a lock file guarantees identical package versions during training. This combination follows best practices for deploy-code workflows by making the training environment reproducible and preventing dependency drift between development, staging, and production.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":79,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is tasked with implementing a simple solution, requiring as little code as possible, to monitor a regression model and ensure that the R2 score does not exceed a certain threshold. If the threshold is exceeded, the solution needs to send an email to a distribution list. Which proposed solution meets the criteria?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Configure Lakehouse Monitoring using the Inference profile type. Create a SQL Alert that runs on a schedule and evaluates if the R2 score in the profile table exceeds the threshold. If so, the Alert should send an email to the configured email notification destination."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Configure Lakehouse Monitoring using the Inference profile type. Create a SQL Query that runs on a schedule and evaluates if the R2 score in the profile table exceeds the threshold. If so, the Alert should send an email to the configured email notification destination."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Configure Lakehouse Monitoring using the Inference profile type. Create a Workflow that runs on a schedule and executes a SQL query that evaluates the R2 score in the profile table. Use Workflow If/Else condition to execute a Notebook Task that sends the email if the R2 score threshold is exceeded."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create a Workflow that runs on a schedule. The Workflow executes a SQL Query that calculates the R2 score. Use Workflow If/Else condition to execute a Notebook Task that sends the email if the R2 score threshold is exceeded."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Lakehouse Monitoring with the Inference profile already computes regression performance metrics such as R2 and stores them in Delta tables. A Databricks SQL Alert can directly evaluate the R2 value against a threshold on a schedule and send an email notification when the condition is met. This requires minimal code and avoids additional workflows or custom logic, making it the simplest and most maintainable solution.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":80,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer wants to use on-demand features to train a model. They have a Python UDF, which relies on data fetched from a FeatureLookup. However, in online serving, the pipeline falls when the lookup ID is not found. Which solution will resolve this issue?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Update the UDF so that it handles both NaNs and None values."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Add a default ID when defining the FeatureLookup, so it does not return None values."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Log the model correctly via the FeatureEngineeringClient, so it automatically evaluates on-demand features."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Register the UDF to Unity Catalog, so None values are handled automatically."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"When an on-demand feature relies on a FeatureLookup, missing lookup keys can result in None values during online serving. Updating the Python UDF to explicitly handle None and NaN values ensures the feature computation is robust to missing lookups, preventing runtime failures while preserving correct behavior during both training and online inference.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":81,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is working with a Spark DataFrame containing 100 million rows of retail transactions across thousands of stores. Each store requires its own demand forecasting model using the same scikit-learn pipeline. They want to train and apply these models in parallel for each store without collecting the data to the driver. Which approach will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use rdd.mapPartitions() to iterate through each store and apply the model logic in Python."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use groupBy(\"store_id\").applyInPandas() to train and apply the model per group using a Pandas UDF."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use pyspark.pandas to call .groupby(\"store_id\").apply() and train the model on each group."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Convert the DataFrame to a Pandas DataFrame using .toPandas() and loop through each store locally."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"groupBy().applyInPandas() enables grouped Pandas UDFs that receive each store's data as a Pandas DataFrame on the executors. This allows training and applying a separate scikit-learn model per store in parallel without collecting data to the driver, making it the correct and scalable approach for large Spark DataFrames.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":82,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has previously built a feature table for model training and inference using a batch mode approach:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q82-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"They have been informed that they now require these features to be available in \"real-time\", with latency on the order of a minute. Their manager has informed them there is now a Kafka stream from which they can stream live data, and they need to have this ingested and available for low-latency feature lookups. Which change to their existing code will achieve this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Change the incoming_df to be a dataframe based on a readStream() from the kafka source, the write_table() method will provide a low-latency lookup on this data."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Change the incoming_df to be a dataframe based on a readStream() from the Kafka source and publish the table as an online table with the streaming option set to True."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Run a triggered workflow to ingest the Kafka data to a dataframe that they can use with their existing write_table() command."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create a custom pyfunc MLflow model which processes results of the Kafka stream for on demand feature calculation."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"To achieve real-time availability with minute-level latency, the feature data must be continuously ingested from Kafka and published to an online table. Using a streaming DataFrame created with readStream from the Kafka source and enabling the online table with streaming allows incremental updates to be synchronized to the online store, supporting low-latency feature lookups for real-time inference.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":83,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer needs to develop a custom anomaly detection model that monitors the internal IT infrastructure of their company. The model takes in compute metrics, logs, and user data and generates a binary prediction. The engineer plans to deploy it as a Databricks Model Serving endpoint. In production there will only be one client calling the endpoint once every 15 seconds. Leadership sees the model as an important part of their operational improvement strategy so maintaining consistent, stable, low latency inference is a requirement while minimizing infrastructure costs. The engineer plans to deploy the endpoint via the MLflow Deployment SDK. Which endpoint config for the MLflow Deployment SDK should the engineer select?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q83-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q83-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q83-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q83-4.jpg"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"With a single client making requests every 15 seconds, the traffic is steady and predictable, and low-latency inference is a strict requirement. Disabling scale-to-zero avoids cold start latency, ensuring consistent response times. Selecting a Small workload size minimizes infrastructure costs while still providing sufficient resources for a lightweight binary classification model, making this configuration the best balance between performance, stability, and cost.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":84,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is training a binary classification model using LogisticRegression in SparkML on a large dataset stored in a Delta table. After fitting the pipeline, they want to evaluate the model's performance using an appropriate metric and scalable method across the distributed test data using the SparkML API. Which model evaluation strategy will suit their needs?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use BinaryClassificationEvaluator with rawPredictionCol parameter, but first convert probability predictions to binary predictions using a UDF to ensure consistent evaluation across different Spark versions."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Convert the DataFrame to RDD and use MulticlassMetrics.confusionMatrix() to calculate precision and recall manually, as this avoids the overhead of DataFrame operations in distributed environments."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the BinaryClassificationEvaluator on the transformed test DataFrame and specify the appropriate metric."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use MulticlassClassificationEvaluator with weightedPrecision metric since it provides more comprehensive evaluation than BinaryClassificationEvaluator for binary problems."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"BinaryClassificationEvaluator is the Spark ML-native, distributed evaluation API designed specifically for binary classification models. It operates directly on the transformed DataFrame produced by the pipeline and efficiently computes scalable metrics such as areaUnderROC or areaUnderPR without requiring data conversion or custom logic, making it the correct and efficient choice for large, distributed datasets.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":85,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is building a Databricks ML pipeline to predict customer churn. The pipeline needs to include automated feature engineering, model training, evaluation, and deployment to a REST API endpoint using MLflow. What is the primary goal of an integration test for this pipeline?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"To verify that the pipeline can process a variety of data formats, even if some stages are skipped or bypassed"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"To ensure that the pipeline's performance meets latency and scalability requirements under simulated production loads"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"To monitor the model's performance after it has been deployed into the production environment"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"To ensure the pipeline runs with all components working together and data flowing correctly between stages"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The primary purpose of an integration test is to validate that all components of the ML pipeline work together as expected. This includes confirming that data flows correctly through feature engineering, training, evaluation, and deployment steps, ensuring the end-to-end pipeline functions properly as a cohesive system.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":86,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer needs to develop fraud detection models with Databricks. They need to ensure seamless collaboration between data engineers and data scientists while maintaining strict governance, version control, and traceability as models progress from development to production. So, they need to choose the Databricks feature that will enable centralized model lineage tracking, cross-workspace access control, and automated synchronization of model versions with their training data. Which Databricks feature will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Mosaic AI Model Serving for deploying models as REST APIs"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Unity Catalog for governing models via a centralized registry with role-based access"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Lakehouse Monitoring for tracking data quality and model drift"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Tracking for logging parameters and metrics during experimentation"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Unity Catalog provides centralized governance for models, including lineage tracking between models and their training data, cross-workspace access control, and consistent version management as models move from development to production. This enables secure collaboration, traceability, and enterprise-grade model governance across teams.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":87,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is tasked with building an automated daily pipeline that updates a customer_features table in Unity Catalog. They have implemented a function, compute_customer_features, that returns a DataFrame with a unique customer_id as the primary key and want to ensure the latest feature values are merged into the table each day. Which code snippet implements this requirement?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q87-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q87-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q87-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q87-4.jpg"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Using write_table with mode set to merge updates existing rows and inserts new ones based on the table's primary key. This ensures that the latest feature values for each customer_id are merged into the existing feature table each day without overwriting the entire table, which is the correct and scalable approach for maintaining up-to-date customer features in Unity Catalog.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":88,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is training a large-scale gradient boosting model using SparkML on a cluster of machines. The training job fails due to memory overflow on a single executor node after processing several iterations. The cluster resources are limited to executor nodes with 16 CPU cores and 64 GB RAM each. The engineer wants to continue training the model without changing hyperparameters or reducing the dataset size. They know Spark's architecture well and want to take advantage of its benefits. Which approach will allow the Machine Learning Engineer to solve this issue?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Increase the number of executor nodes and replicate the entire model on each node, then implement data parallelism to train on different mini-batches simultaneously."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Increase the number of executor nodes and implement data parallelism by partitioning the dataset across executors so each node trains the model on a subset of data."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Increase the number of executor nodes and replicate the entire model on each node, then apply model parallelism to train different parts of the model in parallel."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Increase the number of executor nodes and implement model parallelism by splitting the gradient boosting model across executors so each node trains a part of the model."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Spark ML algorithms, including gradient-boosted trees, are designed around data parallelism. By increasing the number of executor nodes, the dataset can be further partitioned so each executor processes only a subset of the data, reducing per-executor memory pressure while keeping the same model configuration and dataset size. This leverages Spark's distributed architecture without requiring model parallelism or hyperparameter changes.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":89,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following tools can assist in real-time deployments by packaging software with its own application, tools, and libraries?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Cloud-based compute"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"REST APIs"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Containers"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Autoscaling clusters"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these tools"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":["A"],"note":"애플리케이션과 라이브러리를 함께 패키징하는 도구는 컨테이너입니다.","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":90,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following is an obstacle related to streaming machine learning applications?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"End-to-end fault tolerance"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"All of these"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Out-of-order data"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Streaming machine learning applications face multiple challenges, including end-to-end fault tolerance (ensuring recovery from failures without data loss) and out-of-order data (handling events that arrive late or out of sequence). Both are common obstacles in building reliable real-time ML systems.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":91,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is using Lakehouse Monitoring to track the performance of ML models deployed in their environment. They want to monitor significant distributional drift in categorical features with a metric bounded on [0,1] for easy interpretation. Which statistical method should they use?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Jensen-Shannon Distance"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Wasserstein Distance"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Kullback-Leibler Distance"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Kolmogorov-Smirnov Test"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":92,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has trained a credit scoring model and needs to evaluate fairness metrics across different customer segments while maintaining different levels of granularity for business reporting. They need to compute metrics like precision, recall, and demographic parity at the individual feature level (credit_score_range, income_bracket) as well as intersectional slices (combinations of features). The model outputs are stored in a Delta table with prediction probabilities and actual default labels. The engineer wants to systematically evaluate model performance across these various feature slices and granularities. Which approach will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create Lakehouse Monitoring with slicing expressions for individual features and intersection conditions."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Implement custom MLflow evaluation functions that iterate through all possible feature combinations."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use Unity Catalog metric views with dimensions defined for each feature and measures for the fairness metrics."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Build separate Spark DataFrames for each slice and compute metrics using standard DataFrame operations."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Lakehouse Monitoring supports defining slicing expressions on one or more columns, allowing metrics such as precision, recall, and fairness indicators to be computed at both individual feature levels and intersectional combinations. This provides a systematic, scalable, and governed way to evaluate model performance and fairness across multiple granularities directly from Delta tables without custom metric pipelines.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":93,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer needs to build a credit risk model using Databricks. Due to strict data governance, production data cannot be accessed from development or staging environments. To manage MLOps, the engineer uses a \"deploy code\" strategy with separate development, staging, and production environments mapped to different catalogs in Unity Catalog. The CI/CD pipeline automates environment transitions. What is the primary architectural component promoted from staging to production to generate the final, production-ready model in this scenario?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"A containerized image of the complete inference pipeline, including feature transformations."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The CI/CD-approved model training source code, which will be executed in the production environment on the full production dataset."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model version entry in the Unity Catalog that has been transitioned from the \"Staging\" to the \"Production\" alias."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model artifact trained and validated in the staging environment on a subset of the data."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"In a deploy-code strategy, models are not promoted as trained artifacts across environments. Instead, the approved training code is promoted through CI/CD and re-executed in the production environment against production data to produce the final model. This approach satisfies strict data governance requirements by ensuring production data is only accessed in production while maintaining reproducibility and traceability.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":94,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is using joblibspark and MLflowCallback to perform a distributed hyperparameter tuning experiment via Optuna. Assuming they have a single objective they are optimizing for, what will be the default sampler implemented by Optuna?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"GridSampler"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"RandomSampler"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"NSGAIISampler"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"TPESampler"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"For single-objective optimization, Optuna uses the Tree-structured Parzen Estimator (TPE) sampler by default. TPESampler is a Bayesian optimization method that efficiently explores the hyperparameter space by modeling promising parameter regions, making it well suited for scalable hyperparameter tuning with joblibspark and MLflowCallback.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":95,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has created a custom PyFunc model wrapper for a fraud detection system that needs to be registered in Unity Catalog under the schema risk_models in the production catalog. The model requires specific dependencies and must be accessible for governance and version control. The engineer is working on a dedicated cluster with Unity Catalog enabled, but the model registration is failing. Why is the model failing to be registered in this case?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model signature must be provided, as Unity Catalog requires all models to have signatures."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The cluster must be configured with shared access mode instead of dedicated mode."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The PyFunc model must inherit from mlflow.sklearn.Model instead of mlflow.pyfunc.PythonModel."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model artifacts must be stored in DBFS rather than Unity Catalog volumes."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":["B"],"note":"Unity Catalog에 등록하는 모든 모델에는 시그니처가 있어야 합니다. ML 작업은 전용(Dedicated) 액세스 모드 클러스터에서 지원됩니다.","explanation":"Unity Catalog model registration requires the cluster to run in shared access mode. Dedicated (single-user) clusters do not support registering models into Unity Catalog because governance, lineage, and access control enforcement rely on shared-mode execution. As a result, even if Unity Catalog is enabled, model registration will fail when attempted from a dedicated cluster.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":96,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A team is building a machine learning pipeline, which includes feature engineering, model training, model evaluation, and model deployment. Over the last year, the project has grown a lot, with multiple contributors working on it. Currently, they are manually testing their pipelines and functions. However, they are looking to build out a MLOps process and want to organize their unit tests. Which approach to organizing functions and unit tests should they take?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Organize functions and unit tests together in multiple notebooks, grouping them by feature or component."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Write unit tests only after deployment to avoid slowing down development."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Place all functions and their corresponding unit tests in a single notebook or script."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Separate functions into dedicated modules and place all unit tests in a separate test suite or notebook."}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Separating production code into dedicated modules and organizing unit tests in a separate test suite follows standard software engineering and MLOps best practices. This structure improves maintainability, enables automated testing in CI/CD pipelines, supports collaboration among multiple contributors, and scales effectively as the project grows.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":97,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist has trained a regression model using Spark's MLlib to predict housing prices. The model needs to be evaluated to determine how well it predicts on a testing dataset. The Data Scientist wants to use a standard regression metric for the evaluation. They also want to evaluate the model with the least processing overhead required. Which approach will suit their needs?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Convert the testing dataset to a pandas DataFrame and use scikit-learn's root_mean_squared_error function to calculate the root mean squared error on the testing dataset."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the MLlib's RegressionEvaluator with the metricName set to rmse to calculate root mean squared error on the testing dataset."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Implement a Spark UDF to calculate the root mean squared error for each row in the testing dataset and aggregate these values to obtain the overall RMSE score."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the MLlib's RankingEvaluator with the metricName set to rmse to calculate root mean squared error on the testing dataset."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"RegressionEvaluator is the Spark ML-native, distributed evaluation API for regression models. Setting metricName to rmse computes root mean squared error directly on the distributed test DataFrame with minimal overhead, avoiding unnecessary data movement or custom computation logic.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":98,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is setting up Databricks Lakehouse Monitoring to issue alerts based on IoT sensors that monitor various water conditions at a high tech hydroponic farm. Specifically: - pH sensors - Records acidity/alkalinity on a logarithmic scale from 0 to 14 - Pump status monitor - Records the current status of the pump as \"on\", \"off\", \"maintenance\", \"fault\", or \"clean\" - Electrical Conductivity (EC) sensors - Records nutrient level of the water as \"very rich\", \"rich\", \"medium\", \"poor\", \"very poor\" What are the appropriate drift metrics for each of the given sensor types?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"pH: Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS), pump status: Chi-squared, EC sensor: Jensen-Shannon distance."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"pH: Chi-squared, pump status: Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS), EC sensor: Wasserstein distance."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"pH: Jensen-Shannon distance, pump status: Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS), EC sensor: Chi-squared."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"pH: Jensen-Shannon distance, pump status: Wasserstein distance, EC sensor: Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS)."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"pH readings are continuous numerical values, so the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test is appropriate for detecting distribution shifts. Pump status is a nominal categorical variable, making the chi-squared test suitable for identifying changes in category frequencies. Electrical Conductivity levels are categorical with ordered labels, and Jensen-Shannon distance effectively measures distributional changes in such categorical probability distributions.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":99,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has a production AI model that predicts fraudulent transactions in real time. This model is integrated into critical business workflows and any downtime could result in significant financial loss and regulatory penalties. The engineer needs to deploy a new, improved version of the model to production to replace the existing model. The bank's fairness policy requires that all transactions be exposed to the same model at any given time. Additionally, the deployment must meet the following requirements: - Zero downtime: The fraud detection service must remain continuously available to users and downstream systems. - Immediate rollback: If the new model causes issues, you must be able to revert to the previous version instantly. Which deployment strategy meets these requirements?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Canary"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Rolling"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Shadow"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Blue-Green"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Blue-green deployment maintains two fully operational production environments and switches all traffic from the old model to the new model at once. This guarantees zero downtime and ensures that all transactions are handled by the same model version at any given time. If issues arise, traffic can be immediately switched back to the previous environment, enabling instant rollback while meeting strict fairness and availability requirements.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":100,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is preparing a Spark ML pipeline on a customer dataset with numeric features age, annual_income, and transaction_count, each varying widely. Because the chosen algorithm requires inputs normalized to the [0,1] range, they need to apply the appropriate Spark ML transformer to these features. Which Spark ML transformer should the Data Scientist use to scale all features to the [0,1] range?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MaxAbsScaler"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"RobustScaler"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MinMaxScaler"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"StandardScaler"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"MinMaxScaler rescales each numeric feature to a fixed range, typically [0, 1], by subtracting the minimum value and dividing by the feature's range. This makes it the appropriate Spark ML transformer when an algorithm explicitly requires inputs normalized to the [0,1] interval.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":101,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer needs a continuous deployment pipeline for their models hosted on Databricks Model Serving. The deployment automation should execute after a model is trained and registered using MLflow. The goal of the automation is to deploy the latest version of the model from the MLflow Model Registry only if the model can meet the company's strict latency requirements (P95 < 300ms) while serving production traffic. How can the engineer validate that new models meet their latency requirements when served in production?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"A/B test the latest model with Databricks Model Serving so that the latest model receives 5% of production traffic and the current model receives the rest. Use inference tables to calculate P95 latency and verify it is less than 300ms."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Serve the latest model on Databricks Model Serving and use a load testing client to generate requests at the production request rate. Use the load testing client to calculate P95 latency and verify it is less than 300ms."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the MLflow Get Run API to retrieve the model metrics from MLflow Tracking and verify that the model_latency metric is less than 300ms."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the MLflow Get Run API to retrieve the model metrics from MLflow Tracking and verify that the inference_latency metric is less than 300ms."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Validating latency requirements must be done under real production serving conditions. Routing a small percentage of live production traffic to the new model using Databricks Model Serving allows accurate measurement of end-to-end serving latency. Inference tables capture request latency metrics, enabling calculation of the P95 latency and ensuring it meets the strict production threshold before full rollout.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":102,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is setting up a cluster for a deep learning training run, but has a number of settings options to choose from. Their cluster will be reused by other engineers on their team and their datasets vary in size from hundreds of MBs to hundreds of GBs. They need to choose a configuration that allows for performant, stable deep learning training without excessive costs. Which configuration will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Using ML Runtime, use a moderate sized GPU VM for the driver and a variable number of memory optimized CPU VMs for the workers with autoscale enabled"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Using ML Runtime, use a large single node, GPU-enabled VM with auto termination set to 20 minutes to reduce costs"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Using ML Runtime, use a moderately sized CPU VM for the driver and a variable number of GPU enabled VMs for the workers with autoscale enabled"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Using Databricks Runtime, use a moderately sized CPU VM for the driver and a variable number of GPU enabled VMs for the workers with autoscale enabled"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Using the ML Runtime ensures deep learning frameworks and GPU drivers are preconfigured and optimized. A moderately sized CPU driver is sufficient for coordination, while GPU-enabled worker nodes handle the computationally intensive training workload. Enabling autoscaling allows the cluster to efficiently adapt to datasets ranging from hundreds of megabytes to hundreds of gigabytes, providing strong performance without overprovisioning and controlling costs in a shared team environment.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":103,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has a computer vision model in Databricks Model Serving that obscures sensitive data from images. Internal teams use the model throughout the work week when they request access to new files. Recently model users complained that the model takes much longer in the morning. This coincides with when people arrive at work and request files for the day. When the engineer reviews the endpoint health metrics, they see P50 model latency peaks around 9AM at 20 seconds. Request rate also peaks at 9AM at 15 requests/second. The GPU utilization is over 60%, GPU memory usage over 50%, and provisioned concurrency at 4 throughout the day. What can the engineer do to reduce user wait time when request rate peaks at 9AM each morning?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The endpoint is constrained by its ability to handle simultaneous requests. Enable \"scale_to_zero\" on the endpoint to spin up additional endpoints quickly to handle the requests."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The endpoint is constrained by its ability to handle simultaneous requests. Scale the endpoint horizontally by editing the endpoint workload_size from \"Small\" to \"Medium\" to increase the model concurrency."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The endpoint is constrained by its ability to handle simultaneous requests. Define a rate_limit on the endpoint to spread out the requests over the course of the day."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The endpoint is constrained by the GPU. Scale the endpoint vertically by changing the endpoint workload_type from \"GPU_SMALL\" to \"GPU_MEDIUM\"."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The symptoms indicate a concurrency bottleneck during the 9AM traffic spike: request rate increases sharply, latency jumps, and the endpoint is already running at a fixed provisioned concurrency of 4. Increasing workload_size scales the endpoint horizontally to handle more concurrent requests, reducing queueing and bringing down user-perceived wait time during peak demand.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":104,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist has been performing hyperparameter tuning using Ray Tune with grid search. After team discussions, they decide to switch to Bayesian optimization to more efficiently explore the parameter space. Their current code is:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q104-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"How can they implement this change?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q104-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q104-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q104-4.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q104-5.jpg"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Bayesian optimization in Ray Tune requires defining a continuous or discrete search space (such as tune.randint) and explicitly configuring a Bayesian search algorithm. Using tune.randint defines a probabilistic parameter domain, and setting search_alg to BayesOptSearch enables Bayesian optimization to efficiently explore the space based on past trial results, rather than exhaustively enumerating all values as in grid search.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":105,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is building a fraud detection model that needs to use both pre-computed features from a feature table and real-time calculated features based on user location data sent with each inference request. The engineer has created a Python UDF called calculate_distance in Unity Catalog at main.fraud_detection.calculate_distance that computes the distance between a transaction location and the user's current location. The feature table main.fraud_detection.user_features contains historical user spending patterns with primary key user_id. The engineer has written the following code to implement this scenario:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q105-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which benefit of this implementation approach makes it suited to the real-time fraud detection use case?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Unity Catalog registry automatically creates REST API endpoints for UDF functions used in feature computation."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The FeatureLookup function avoids the need for joining the full table main.fraud_detection.user_features with training_set increasing efficiency."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The model automatically performs feature lookup and computation during inference without additional serving code."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The FeatureFunction caches computed distance values in the online store to improve inference latency for location pairs."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"By defining both FeatureLookup and FeatureFunction objects in the training set and logging the model with the FeatureEngineeringClient, the feature logic is packaged with the model. During inference, Databricks automatically performs feature lookups from the feature table and computes the on-demand distance feature using request-time inputs, without requiring any additional custom serving or feature-joining code. This makes the approach well suited for real-time fraud detection.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":106,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is developing a model training pipeline on Databricks and needs to track custom performance metrics during training. They want to log a custom evaluation score (team_score), a single hyperparameter, and a confusion matrix plot as part of their MLflow experiment. Which code snippet correctly logs all three types of information in MLflow?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_param(\"max_depth\", 5) mlflow.log_metric(\"team_score\", 0.92) mlflow.log_artifact(open(\"confusion_matrix.png\"))"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_param(\"max_depth\", 5) mlflow.log_metric(\"team_score\", 0.92) mlflow.log_artifact(\"confusion_matrix.png\")"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_param(\"max_depth\", \"5\") mlflow.log_metric(\"team_score\", \"0.92\") mlflow.log_artifact(\"confusion_matrix.png\")"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_metric({\"team_score\": 0.92}) mlflow.log_param([\"max_depth\", 5]) mlflow.log_file(\"confusion_matrix.png\")"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"This snippet correctly uses MLflow's APIs to log each item in its expected form: a single hyperparameter with log_param, a numeric custom metric with log_metric, and a file-based artifact such as a confusion matrix image by passing its file path to log_artifact. This is the standard and correct way to track parameters, metrics, and artifacts in an MLflow experiment.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":107,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is using Lakehouse Monitoring to track the performance of ML models deployed in their environment. They want to monitor significant distributional drift in categorical features with a metric bounded on [0,1] for easy interpretation. Which statistical method should they use?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Jensen-Shannon Distance"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Wasserstein Distance"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Kolmogorov-Smirnov Test"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Kullback-Leibler Distance"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Jensen-Shannon Distance is well suited for measuring distributional drift in categorical features. It is symmetric, numerically stable, and bounded between 0 and 1, which makes it easy to interpret and ideal for monitoring categorical feature drift in Lakehouse Monitoring.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":108,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is considering moving their functions and unit tests from notebooks into separate Python files (e.g., modules and test scripts) to take advantage of the numerous benefits of this approach like automated execution, code reusability, and version control. Which challenge should the engineer consider with this approach?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"This change would prevent the use of any non-Python environments like Scala."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Separating code and tests often leads to decreased reliability and poor code quality."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Managing a more complex project structure can be harder to maintain and navigate."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It makes functions harder to import and reuse across different notebooks."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Moving code and unit tests into separate Python modules introduces a more structured project layout, which can increase complexity. Engineers must manage directories, dependencies, and imports carefully, making the project slightly harder to navigate and maintain compared to simple notebook-based workflows, especially for teams new to this structure.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":109,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has automated a model retraining job in Databricks. Each scheduled run trains multiple candidate models with new sales data and logs all runs with MLflow. The goal is to select and register the best-performing model at the end of each cycle to ensure optimal forecast accuracy. Which approach will meet this goal?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Register the first model that has an evaluation metric better than the previously deployed model."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Register the best model based on the primary evaluation metric."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Register the model with the lowest training loss regardless of validation metrics."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Register all models from the run since all of them are trained on newer data and hence will be more accurate."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Selecting and registering the model that performs best on the primary evaluation metric ensures that only the highest-quality model is promoted at each retraining cycle. This approach aligns with MLOps best practices by basing promotion decisions on objective performance criteria rather than training order or assumptions about data freshness.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":110,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is conducting hyperparameter tuning for multiple XGBoost models using Ray Tune on Databricks. They want to integrate MLflow tracking to monitor their experiments and need to ensure proper authentication. The engineer has Ray 2.41 installed and wants to use both Ray Tune and MLflow together in their distributed tuning workflow. They have to configure Databricks to run the hyperparameter optimization with MLflow integration. Which set of configuration steps will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Install the MLflow Ray plugin using %pip install mlflow-ray and configure the workspace connection."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Configure DATABRICKS_HOST and DATABRICKS_TOKEN environment variables before calling setup_ray_cluster()."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Enable MLflow autologging with mlflow.ray.autolog() and set the tracking server URI."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Set MLFLOW_TRACKING_URI and MLFLOW_EXPERIMENT_ID environment variables before initializing Ray."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"When using Ray Tune with MLflow on Databricks, Ray workers must be able to authenticate back to the Databricks workspace to log runs to MLflow Tracking. Setting the DATABRICKS_HOST and DATABRICKS_TOKEN environment variables before initializing the Ray cluster ensures all Ray processes can securely communicate with Databricks and correctly log MLflow experiments during distributed hyperparameter tuning.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":111,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer needs to deploy a production ML workflow that includes an MLflow experiment for tracking model training runs, a registered model in Unity Catalog for version management, and a model serving endpoint for real-time inference. The team requires a unified configuration approach that ensures consistent deployment across development and production environments while adhering to infrastructure-as-code best practices. Which approach should the Machine Learning Engineer use to define all three components together?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use MLflow's deployment tools to create individual deployment configurations for each component, then orchestrate them using Databricks Jobs."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use Terraform to provision the infrastructure and then manually configure each ML component through the Databricks UI."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Define experiments, registered_models, and model_serving_endpoints resources in a Databricks Asset Bundle (DAB) configuration file."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create separate REST API calls for each component and run them sequentially using a shell script."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Databricks Asset Bundles allow experiments, Unity Catalog-registered models, and model serving endpoints to be defined declaratively in a single configuration. This provides a unified, version-controlled, infrastructure-as-code approach that ensures consistent deployment across environments and aligns with MLOps best practices.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":112,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer uses Lakehouse Monitoring to track their credit scoring model's performance. The existing profile metrics table contains three aggregate metrics: - adefault_risk_score - payment_history_score - credit_utilization_score They need to: 1. Create a composite risk rating that combines these three scores using weights of 0.5, 0.3, and 0.2 respectively. 2. Monitor drift of this composite score against an established baseline. Which approach should be used to implement both requirements within Lakehouse Monitoring?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create two separate aggregate metrics: one for the composite score calculation and another for drift detection."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use a scheduled notebook to calculate the composite score and manually insert both the score and drift values into the profile metrics table."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create a derived metric for the composite_risk_rating calculation and create a drift metric on this derived metric."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create an aggregate metric for composite_risk_rating and configure a separate drift metric to monitor changes."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Lakehouse Monitoring supports derived metrics that are computed from existing profile metrics using custom expressions. By defining a derived metric for the composite_risk_rating using the specified weights, the composite score becomes a first-class metric in the monitoring framework. A drift metric can then be directly configured on this derived metric to compare current values against the baseline, fulfilling both the composite calculation and drift monitoring requirements in a native, governed way.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":113,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has deployed a fraud detection model that processes 10,000 transactions per hour. The model was trained on data from Q1 2024, but it's now Q4 2024. The ML team notices three concerning trends: (1) the model's precision has dropped from 92% to 78% over the past month, (2) the average transaction amount in recent data has increased from $ 150 to $ 220 and (3) the relationship between transaction frequency and fraud likelihood has weakened significantly due to new payment methods being introduced. The engineer needs to implement a monitoring solution that can detect the root cause of the performance degradation, identify why the precision dropped, and be able to do this at the scale needed. Which monitoring pipeline component will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model Health Monitoring Pipeline because it ensures the infrastructure can handle the 10,000 transactions per hour load."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Drift Detection Pipeline because it can identify both the data drift (transaction amount changes) and concept drift (weakened relationship between features and target)."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Logging and Monitoring Pipeline because it captures all prediction requests and can identify patterns in the fraud predictions."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model Performance Monitoring Pipeline because it directly tracks precision metrics and can alert when thresholds are breached."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A drift detection pipeline is designed to diagnose the root causes of model performance degradation at scale. It can detect data drift, such as changes in the distribution of transaction amounts, and concept drift, where the relationship between input features and the fraud label changes due to new payment methods. This directly explains why precision dropped and provides actionable insight beyond simply observing metric degradation.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":114,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which MLflow component is used to log parameters, metrics, and artifacts during model training?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model Registry"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Tracking"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Projects"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Models"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"MLflow Tracking records experiment runs including: parameters metrics artifacts (models, plots, etc.)","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":115,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which stage in the MLflow Model Registry is typically used for models currently serving production traffic?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Staging"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Production"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Archived"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"In MLflow Model Registry stages: Staging -> testing before release Production -> serving real users Archived -> retired models","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":116,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Why are Delta tables often used to store machine learning features?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"They support schema enforcement and time travel"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"They allow faster GPU training"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"They reduce model size"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"They replace Spark DataFrames"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Delta Lake provides: ACID transactions time travel schema enforcement These are essential for reproducible ML pipelines.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":117,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"What is the main purpose of the Databricks Feature Store?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Storing raw data"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Centralizing reusable ML features"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replacing Spark ML pipelines"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Storing trained models"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Feature Store allows teams to: share features avoid training/serving skew maintain feature lineage.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":118,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"In a Spark ML Pipeline, what is the role of a Transformer?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Fits data"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Converts datasets into new datasets"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Trains models"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Evaluates models"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A Transformer takes a dataset and transforms it into another dataset.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":119,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is converting a Hyperopt-based hyperparameter tuning process from manual MLflow logging to MLflow Autologging. They notice that not all details and objects are automatically logged, and they will need to manually log some things. Which of the following will need to be manually logged when performing nested runs with Hyperopt and MLflow Autologging?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Trial models"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Trial status"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Best trial evaluation metric"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Hyperparameter values"}]},{"key":"E","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Evaluation metrics"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"When using MLflow Autologging with Hyperopt and nested runs, the best trial evaluation metric is not automatically logged and must be logged manually. Autologging captures trial-level details like hyperparameters and evaluation metrics, but summarizing and logging the overall best trial's result is a manual responsibility of the engineer.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":120,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist wants to track the runs of their random forest model. The data scientist is changing the number of trees and the maximum depth of the trees in the forest across each run. They write the following code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q120-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which Python object type does params need to be an instance of?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"array"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"PySpark DataFrame"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"dict"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"list"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The params variable must be a dictionary (dict) because mlflow.log_params() expects a dictionary where each key-value pair represents a parameter name and its corresponding value. Additionally, the model instantiation RandomForestRegressor(**params) also requires params to be a dictionary to unpack the parameters correctly.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":121,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist wants to examine the data in the Feature Store table table from the database dev as a Spark DataFrame. They have access to Feature Store Client fs. Which line of code can be used to get the data from table as a Spark DataFrame?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.get_table(\"table)"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.read_table(\"dev.table\")"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.create_table(\"dev.table\")"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.get_table(\"dev.table\")"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"To read data from a Feature Store table as a Spark DataFrame, the correct method is fs.read_table(\"dev.table\"). This retrieves the contents of the table in the dev database using the Feature Store Client (fs) and returns it as a Spark DataFrame.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":122,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has computed updated rows that contain new feature values for primary keys already stored in the Feature Store table features. The updated feature values are stored in the DataFrame features_df. They want to update the rows in features if the associated primary key is in features_df. If a row's primary key is not in features_df, they want the row to remain unchanged in features. Which code block can they use to perform this task using the Feature Store Client fs?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q122-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q122-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q122-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q122-4.jpg"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"To update existing rows based on primary keys while leaving other rows unchanged, the correct mode to use with fs.write_table() is \"merge\". This performs an upsert operation - updating rows where keys match and keeping others intact - making it ideal for updating feature values in a Feature Store table.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":123,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has developed a machine learning pipeline that produces a scikit-learn model and computes the RMSE rmse. MAE mae, and R-squared r2 values for the test set. They now want to log these values with the MLflow run. These values are stored in the dictionary metrics. They run the following code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q123-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"The code block produces an error. Which changes to the code block will successfully complete the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace mlflow.log_metric with mlflow.sklearn.log_metric"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace metrics with model"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace log_metric with log_metrics"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace metrics with rmse, mae, r2"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The method mlflow.log_metric() logs a single metric, while mlflow.log_metrics() is used to log multiple metrics at once from a dictionary. Since metrics is a dictionary containing rmse, mae, and r2, the correct function is mlflow.log_metrics(metrics).","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":124,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist is utilizing MLflow to track their machine learning experiments. After completing a run with run ID run_id for the experiment with experiment ID exp_id, the data scientist wants to programmatically return the logged metrics for run_id. They have an active MLflow Client client and an active Spark session spark. Which lines of code can be used to return the logged metrics for run_id?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.get_run(exp_id.run_id).data.metrics"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q124-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"client.get_run(run_id).data.metrics"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"spark.read.format(\"mlflow-run\").load(run_id)"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The correct way to retrieve logged metrics for a specific run using the MLflow Client is client.get_run(run_id).data.metrics. This returns a dictionary of all metrics logged for that run. The method in the image (mlflow.search_runs(...)) is for querying across multiple runs, not for accessing a specific run's metrics.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":125,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to move their model version model_version for the MLflow Model Registry model `model` from the Staging stage to the Production stage using MLflow Client client. Which code block can they use to accomplish the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q125-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q125-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q125-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q125-4.jpg"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The correct method for transitioning a model version to a new stage using the MLflow Client is client.transition_model_version_stage(name, version, stage). This updates the stage of a specific model version in the Model Registry, such as moving it from \"Staging\" to \"Production\". The stage parameter specifies the target stage only -- there is no from argument.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":126,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has created a webhook with the following code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q126-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which code block will trigger this webhook to run the associate job?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q126-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q126-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q126-4.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q126-5.jpg"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The webhook in the first image is configured to trigger on the MODEL_VERSION_TRANSITIONED_TO_PRODUCTION event. The chosen code uses client.transition_model_version_stage(...) to move the model version to \"Production\", which matches the event type in the webhook and will correctly trigger the associated job.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":127,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following statements about built-in library-specific MLflow Model flavors is true?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Built-in library-specific flavors are required for model signature use"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Built-in library-specific flavors allow models to be exported as library objects"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Built-in library-specific flavors allow models to be used with any library"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Built-in library-specific flavors can only be used for logging models"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Built-in library-specific MLflow model flavors (e.g., mlflow.sklearn, mlflow.xgboost) allow models to be exported and later loaded as native library objects, enabling seamless reuse with the original libraries for inference or further training.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":128,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which tool can be used to automatically start a testing Job when a new version of an MLflow Model Registry model is registered?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Model Registry UI"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Client API"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Model Registry Webhooks"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow REST API"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"MLflow Model Registry Webhooks can be configured to automatically trigger actions - such as running a testing job - when events occur, like registering a new model version. This enables automation in CI/CD workflows for machine learning models.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":129,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has developed a model and registered it using the FeatureStoreClient fs. The model has model URI model_uri. The engineer now needs to perform batch inference on the training set logged with the model, but a few of the feature values in the column spend have since been updated and are present in the customer-level Spark DataFrame spark_df. The customer_id column is the primary key of spark_df and the training set used when training and logging the model. Which code block can be used to compute predictions for the training set while overwriting its old spend values with the new spend values from spark_df?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.score_batch(model_uri, spark_df)"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"fs.score_model(model_uri, spark_df)"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"df = fs.get_updated_feature(spark_df, model=uri) fs.score_batch(model_uri, df)"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"df = fs.get_updated_features(spark_df) fs.score_batch(model_uri, df)"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":["D"],"note":"fs.score_batch(model_uri, spark_df)에 넘긴 DataFrame에 이미 피처 컬럼(spend)이 있으면 그 값으로 저장된 피처를 덮어써 추론합니다. get_updated_features 메서드는 없습니다.","explanation":"To perform batch inference while incorporating updated feature values (like spend) from a DataFrame (spark_df), the correct approach is to use fs.get_updated_features(spark_df) to refresh the relevant features based on the primary key (customer_id), then score the model using fs.score_batch(...). This ensures predictions are made with the latest data.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":130,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has deployed a model recommender using MLflow Model Serving. They now want to query the version of that model that is in the Staging stage of the MLflow Model Registry. Which model URI can be used to query the described model version?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The version number of the model version in Production is necessary to complete this task."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"https://<databricks-instance>/model-serving/recommender/Staging/invocations"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It is not possible to query a model version in the Staging stage."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"https://<databricks-instance>/model/recommender/Staging/invocations"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"To query a model version in a specific stage (like Staging) using MLflow Model Serving on Databricks, the correct model URI format is: https://<databricks-instance>/model/<model-name>/<stage>/invocations So, for the model named recommender in the Staging stage, the correct URI is: https://<databricks-instance>/model/recommender/Staging/invocations.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":131,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is working on a fraud detection machine learning application. When a transaction is made with a credit card, the machine learning application will immediately process the data and make a prediction to determine whether or not to approve the transaction based on the probability that the transaction is fraudulent. Which deployment strategy can be used to meet these requirements?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Edge/on-device"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Real-time"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Batch"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Streaming"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Real-time deployment is required for applications that must provide immediate predictions, such as fraud detection during a credit card transaction. This ensures that the system can instantly assess risk and approve or deny the transaction without delay.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":132,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has developed a random forest model using scikit-learn and registered the model using MLflow. They now want to deploy that model in parallel. Which of the following operations can they use to create a function they can use to deploy the registered scikit-learn model in parallel?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It is not possible to deploy a scikit-learn model in parallel."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.spark.pandas_udf"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.spark.udf"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.pyfunc.spark.udf"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":["C"],"note":"등록된 scikit-learn 모델을 Spark에서 병렬 추론용 UDF로 만들 때는 mlflow.pyfunc.spark_udf(보기 D의 mlflow.pyfunc.spark.udf)를 씁니다. mlflow.sklearn에는 spark_udf가 없습니다.","explanation":"The correct operation is mlflow.sklearn.spark_udf, which creates a Spark UDF from a registered scikit-learn model. This allows the model to be applied in parallel across a Spark DataFrame, enabling scalable batch inference using Spark.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":133,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which tool can assist in real-time deployments by packaging software with its own application, tools, and libraries?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Click"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Structured Streaming"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Docker"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Flask"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Docker is a containerization tool that packages software along with its dependencies, tools, and libraries into a single container. This ensures consistency across environments and is highly useful for real-time deployments, enabling scalable and portable model serving.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":134,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"What does an Estimator do in Spark ML?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Evaluates model performance"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Trains a model using data"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Transforms a dataset"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Stores features"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"An Estimator learns from data and produces a Transformer.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":135,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which Spark ML class supports automated hyperparameter tuning?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"ParamGridBuilder"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"CrossValidator"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"PipelineModel"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"BinaryClassificationEvaluator"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"CrossValidator performs: parameter search k-fold cross validation.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":136,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"What is the purpose of ParamGridBuilder?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model deployment"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Creating hyperparameter combinations"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Feature storage"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Tracking metrics"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"ParamGridBuilder creates parameter search grids for tuning.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":137,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which MLflow command logs a trained model?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.run()"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.start_run()"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_model()"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.register_model()"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"mlflow.log_model() saves the model artifact within the run.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":138,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which MLflow function registers a model to the registry?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_param()"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.register_model()"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.deploy_model()"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.create_model()"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"mlflow.register_model() promotes a model into the Model Registry.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":139,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"In order to connect an MLflow Model Registry Webhook to a Databricks Job, the Job ID must be provided to the code block used to create the webhook. Which approach can be used to obtain a Databricks Job ID?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Job ID field returned in a query using the Jobs API"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Job ID field in the Jobs page in the UI"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The Job ID field in the Job details section of the specific Job page in the UI"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"All of these approaches"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A Databricks Job ID can be obtained in multiple ways - it is displayed directly in the Jobs page, in the Job details section of a specific Job, and can also be retrieved programmatically through the Databricks Jobs API. Any of these methods can be used to supply the Job ID when configuring an MLflow Model Registry Webhook.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":140,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer needs a python_model to access a collection of files using its load_context operation. The collection of files being accessed by python_model.load_context needs to be saved when the model is being logged. A dictionary of the names and paths of these files is properly stored in my_dict. The machine learning engineer has written the following incomplete block of code:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q140-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which lines of code can be used to fill in the blank to successfully complete the code block to accomplish the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"artifacts"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these lines of code can be used to successfully complete the code block"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"context"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"model_context"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"When logging a custom Python model with mlflow.pyfunc.log_model(), the artifacts parameter is used to specify a dictionary (my_dict) of file names and their paths that the model may need to access during loading or inference. These files are stored as model artifacts and become available to the model through the load_context method.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":141,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has developed the following custom model class with preprocessing logic to combine two columns:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q141-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"However, instances of this class are unable to compute predictions. Which set of changes will update the class so predictions can be computed while continuing to apply the preprocessing logic?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace model_input.copy() with self.preprocess_input(model_input.copy()) in the preprocess_input method"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Remove the self.rf_model = rf_model line from the fit method"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace model_input.copy() with self.preprocess_input(model_input.copy()) in the predict metho d"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace self.rf_model.predict(input) with self.predict(input) in the predict method"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The issue is that the predict() method does not apply the same preprocessing as the fit() method. During training, the model uses preprocessed data (via self.preprocess_input()), but during prediction, it directly uses raw input. This mismatch causes prediction errors because the model expects preprocessed input. By updating the predict() method to call self.preprocess_input(model_input.copy()), both training and prediction use consistent feature transformations, allowing predictions to be computed successfully.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":142,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is migrating a machine learning pipeline to use Databricks Machine Learning. The pipeline needs to automatically refresh its model each time it runs. They are using the following code block as part of their solution:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q142-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Assuming that this is the first time that the model is being run, which statement describes the impact of the registered_model_name=model_name parameter?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It registers the new version of the existing model_name model in the MLflow Model Registry."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It registers a new model called model_name in the MLflow Model Registry."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It avoids the need to specify the model name in the subsequent required call to mlflow.register_model."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It identifies the name of the logged model in the MLflow Experiment."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The parameter registered_model_name=model_name in mlflow.pyfunc.log_model() automatically creates and registers a new model in the MLflow Model Registry if it doesn't already exist. Since this is the first time the model is being run, MLflow will register a new model named model_name and log the current model as version 1 of that registered model.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":143,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer wants to delete an active MLflow Model Registry Webhook with webhook ID webhook_id for a specific model. They are using the following code block:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q143-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which change does the machine learning engineer need to make to this code block so it will successfully accomplish the task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace list with delete in the endpoint URL"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"There are no necessary changes"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace list with webhooks in the endpoint URL"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace DELETE with POST in the call to http_request"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"To delete a webhook using the MLflow REST API, the correct endpoint is: /api/2.0/mlflow/registry-webhooks/delete In the provided code, the endpoint incorrectly uses \"list\", which retrieves webhooks rather than deleting them. The HTTP method \"DELETE\" is correct, so the only necessary change is to replace list with delete in the endpoint URL.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":144,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is migrating a machine learning pipeline to use Databricks Machine Learning. The pipeline needs to automatically refresh its model each time it runs. The project is attached to the existing model model_name in the MLflow Model Registry. They are using the following code block as part of their solution:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q144-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which statement describes the impact of the registered_model_name=model_name parameter and argument given that model_name already exists in the MLflow Model Registry?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It identifies the name of the logged model in the MLflow Experiment."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It avoids the need to specify the model name in the subsequent required call to mlflow.register_model."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It registers the new version of the model_name model in the MLflow Model Registry."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"It registers a new model called model_name in the MLflow Model Registry."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"When using registered_model_name=model_name in mlflow.spark.log_model(), MLflow automatically registers the logged model under the specified model name. If that model name already exists in the MLflow Model Registry, MLflow creates a new version of that existing registered model rather than a new model entry. This enables automatic versioning and continuous model refresh with each pipeline run.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":145,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following is an obstacle related to streaming machine learning applications?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"End-to-end fault tolerance"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Out-of-order data"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"All of these"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Streaming machine learning applications face multiple challenges, including end-to-end fault tolerance (ensuring recovery from failures without data loss) and out-of-order data (handling events that arrive late or out of sequence). Both are common obstacles in building reliable real-time ML systems.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":146,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has registered a Spark ML model in the MLflow Model Registry using the Spark ML model flavor with a model_uri. Which operation can be used to load the model as a Spark ML object for batch deployment?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.spark.read_model(model_uri)"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.pyfunc.load_model(model_uri)"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.spark.load_model(model_uri)"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.sklearn.load_model(model_uri)"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"To load a model that was logged using the Spark ML flavor, the correct operation is mlflow.spark.load_model(model_uri). This restores the model as a Spark ML object, preserving its original structure and functionality for batch inference or further processing in Spark environments.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":147,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has deployed a model recommender using MLflow Model Serving. They now want to query the version of that model that is in the Production stage of the MLflow Model Registry. Which of the following model URls can be used to query the described model version?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"https:///model/recommender/stage-production/invocations"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The version number of the model version in Production is necessary to complete this task."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"https:///model-serving/recommender/Production/invocations"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"https:///model/recommender/Production/invocations"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"In MLflow Model Serving, the correct URL pattern to query a specific stage of a registered model is https:///model/<model_name>/<stage>/invocations Thus, to query the model named recommender in the Production stage, the proper endpoint is https:///model/recommender/Production/invocations. This allows direct access to the active production version without needing its version number.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":148,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has a machine learning pipeline where predictions are updated annually. The final prediction dataset contains millions of rows, and that dataset is irregularly accessed. Which solution should the machine learning engineer use to maintain cost efficiency?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Cloud-based object storage"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Cloud-based in-memory instance of a Spark DataFrame"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Cloud-based low latency database"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"On-premises low-latency database"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"For data that is large in size, infrequently accessed, and updated only periodically, cloud-based object storage (such as AWS S3, Azure Blob Storage, or Google Cloud Storage) is the most cost-efficient option. It offers durable, scalable, and inexpensive storage compared to in-memory or low-latency databases, which are optimized for frequent access and real-time performance rather than long-term, infrequent retrieval.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":149,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer would like to compute predictions on inference data as it becomes available through the pipeline in microbatches. The predictions should be stored in a table for query later. Which deployment strategy can the engineer use?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Real-time"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Streaming"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Batch"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Edge/on-device"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A streaming deployment strategy processes incoming data continuously or in microbatches, allowing predictions to be computed as new data arrives. The results can then be written to a table for later querying. This approach balances near-real-time inference with manageable resource use, making it ideal for scenarios where data flows steadily through a pipeline.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":150,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineering team has decided that they need to have predictions be made available for querying in continuous, equal-sized increments. A computation can be included in one of the increments when all of its feature values are in the inference Spark DataFrame. Which of the following tools can be used to provide this type of continuous inference?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Delta Lake"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Spark UDFs"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Structured Streaming"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Structured Streaming in Apache Spark enables continuous inference by processing incoming data in microbatches or continuous increments. It ensures that each computation occurs only when all required feature values are available in the inference DataFrame, supporting real-time or near-real-time prediction pipelines with consistent, equal-sized processing intervals.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":151,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist has developed a model to predict whether or not it will rain using the expected temperature and expected cloud coverage. However, the proportion of days where it actually rains has increased dramatically from the proportion in the data on which the model was trained. Which type of drift is present in the above scenario?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Feature drift"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Label drift"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Prediction drift"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Concept drift"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Label drift occurs when the distribution of the target variable (label) changes over time while the relationship between features and the label remains the same. In this scenario, the proportion of days when it rains (the label) has changed significantly compared to the training data, indicating label drift.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":152,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following is a drawback associated with using Jensen-Shannon (JS) distance for numeric feature drift detection?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"All of these reasons"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"JS requires a manual threshold or cutoff determinations"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"JS is not robust when working with large datasets"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these reasons"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A key drawback of using Jensen-Shannon (JS) distance for numeric feature drift detection is that it requires manual threshold tuning to determine what level of divergence indicates significant drift. This makes automation and consistent interpretation challenging, especially across features or datasets with differing distributions.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":153,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which of the following can be used to compare the relative prevalence of specific values in a single categorical variable between two datasets or time periods?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Contingency tables"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Number of unique values"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Mode"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Jensen-Shannon distance"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Contingency tables (also known as cross-tabulations) display the frequency distribution of categorical variables, allowing comparison of how specific category values occur across two datasets or time periods. This makes them effective for identifying categorical feature drift or shifts in value prevalence.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":154,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"What tool helps avoid feature mismatch between training and inference?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Projects"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Feature Store"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model Registry"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Spark SQL"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Feature Store ensures: same feature computation logic consistency between training and serving.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":155,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which MLflow feature helps reproduce training runs?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model Serving"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Projects"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Tracking"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Delta Sharing"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Tracking logs: parameters code version metrics artifacts making runs reproducible.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":156,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Why is Apache Spark useful for machine learning training?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"GPU rendering"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Distributed data processing"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Web serving"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Data visualization"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Spark processes large distributed datasets efficiently.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":157,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which evaluator is used for binary classification?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"RegressionEvaluator"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MulticlassEvaluator"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"BinaryClassificationEvaluator"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"ClusteringEvaluator"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"BinaryClassificationEvaluator computes metrics like: AUC areaUnderPR.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":158,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which component manages model versions?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"MLflow Tracking"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model Registry"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Feature Store"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Spark ML"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Model Registry handles: versioning stage transitions governance.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":159,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer has implemented a numeric drift monitoring solution by examining trends in the summary statistics of input variables. However, the engineer's stakeholders would like a more robust monitoring solution. Which of the following can provide a more robust drift monitoring solution for numeric feature variables?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"None of these can provide more robust drift monitoring than summary statistics"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Statistical tests"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Oversampling"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Correlations"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Statistical tests (such as the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test or Wasserstein distance) provide a more robust and quantitative method for detecting numeric feature drift compared to simple summary statistics. These tests compare the full distributions of features between datasets, making them more sensitive to subtle changes in data behavior over time.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":160,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Feature drift occurs when there is a change in which element?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The distribution of the predicted target given by the model"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The distribution of an input variable"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The distribution of a target variable"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The relationship between input variables and target variables"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Feature drift refers to a change in the distribution of one or more input features over time, even if the target variable and model relationship remain stable. This can degrade model performance because the model was trained on a different feature distribution than it encounters during inference.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":161,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Concept drift is when there is a change in which element?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The relationship between input variables and target variables"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The distribution of an input variable"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The distribution of the predicted target given by the model"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"The distribution of a target variable"}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Concept drift occurs when the underlying relationship between input features and the target variable changes over time. This means that even if the feature and label distributions remain stable, the way features influence the target (the predictive relationship) has shifted, leading to reduced model accuracy unless retraining occurs.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":162,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist needs to analyze drift detection results from Databricks Lakehouse Monitoring. The system has generated both profile metrics and drift metrics tables. The scientist needs to identify baseline drift in numerical features by comparing current data against a baseline from 6 months ago. Which combination of table columns and values indicates baseline drift in a numerical feature?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"window_cmp pointing to baseline time window and tv_distance > 0.5 with any drift_type value."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"drift_type = \"BASELINE\", chi_squared_test.p_value < 0.05, and js_distance > 0.2."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"drift_type = \"BASELINE\", ks_test.p_value < 0.05, and wasserstein_distance > 0.1."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"log_type = \"BASELINE\" in profile metrics table with population_stability_index > 0.2 in drift metrics table."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Baseline drift is identified in the drift metrics table by setting drift_type to BASELINE, and for numerical features the drift statistics are the KS test (ks_test with a p-value indicating a statistically significant distribution change) and a numeric distance metric such as wasserstein_distance.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":163,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has a large dataset with a customer_region column and wants to train separate models for each region, then generate predictions. They need to parallelize this group-specific model training process using Databricks and the Pandas Function API. Which approach will implement this solution?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use collect() to gather all data and process regions using standard pandas groupby."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use groupBy(\"customer_region\") and apply a training function with applyInPandas()."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use foreachBatch() to sequentially process each region's data."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use mapInPandas() to apply the training function across all partitions without grouping."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The Pandas Function API supports parallel, group-specific processing by using groupBy on the grouping column and applyInPandas to execute a custom training function independently for each group. This enables separate models to be trained per region in parallel across the cluster, with each function invocation receiving only the data for its group.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":164,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A machine learning engineer is developing a recommendation system for online content. They are using the Databricks Feature Store to store features for training and inference. Which unit test should they create?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Test that Pandas DataFrame operations work as expected"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Test feature transformation functions"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Test MLflow Model Registry operations"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Test Feature Store lookup during inference"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"When using the Databricks Feature Store, the most important unit tests validate that feature transformation logic produces correct and consistent outputs. Testing feature transformation functions ensures that features written to the Feature Store are accurate and reliable for both training and inference, independent of infrastructure or serving mechanisms.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":165,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A data scientist is building a model to predict which communication channel (Phone, SMS, Email, or Post) is most likely to be effective for a given customer. Which model type is suited to this task?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Linear Regression"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"ARIMA"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Logistic Regression"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Softmax Classifier"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"This task requires predicting one class from multiple mutually exclusive categories. A softmax classifier is designed for multiclass classification problems and outputs a probability distribution across all possible communication channels, allowing selection of the most likely effective channel.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":166,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is tasked with developing models to forecast product demand. The company offers 5000 different product types, and the Data Scientist must generate weekly forecasts for each type. They have access to two years of historical purchase data and are given ample project budget. For their next project, they want to build 5000 separate Random Forest models, one for each product type. They aim to train all the models as quickly as possible with minimal setup. Which approach meets these requirements?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the DeepSpeed library to distribute the data by product across different nodes to enable the parallel training of multiple models."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create a Databricks Workflow with 5000 tasks. Each task is configured to accept a product ID as a parameter which will then train a model based on the specified product ID."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Leverage the pandas function API (Grouped map) to group the data by product type and apply a custom model training function to each group."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the RandomForest method from MLlib. This will leverage Spark's parallel processing capability to train 5000 different models."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The pandas function API with grouped map allows data to be grouped by product type and applies a custom training function independently to each group. This approach enables massive parallelism across the cluster with minimal orchestration or setup, making it well suited for rapidly training thousands of independent models in parallel.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":167,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist at an online gaming company is creating a model to predict player churn. The company currently collects terabytes of player activity logs daily, which are stored in Databricks and processed for daily reporting. The Data Scientist has completed feature engineering and the resulting data is saved as a Delta Table with a size of 500GB. They need to next build the model for the most performant and cost-effective performance for Databricks. Which approach will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Load the feature data as a Spark DataFrame and train the model using Spark's DeepspeedTorchDistributor on a multi-node Databricks cluster."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Load the feature data as a pandas DataFrame and train the model using scikit-learn's RandomForestClassifier on a multi-node Databricks cluster."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Load the feature data as a pandas DataFrame and train the model using scikit-learn's RandomForestClassifier on a single-node Databricks cluster."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Load the feature data as a Spark DataFrame and train the model using SparkML's RandomForestClassifier on a multi-node Databricks cluster."}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A 500GB Delta Table is far beyond what is practical to load into a single pandas DataFrame, and scaling pandas-based scikit-learn training across nodes is not the right fit for this workload. Using a Spark DataFrame with Spark ML's RandomForestClassifier leverages distributed data processing and distributed model training on a multi-node cluster, which is the most performant and cost-effective approach for large tabular datasets in Databricks.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":168,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has deployed a customer churn prediction model to production three months ago. The model serves real-time predictions via a Databricks endpoint with inference logging enabled. They notice declining model accuracy in recent weeks and suspect data drift in customer demographics. They need to implement monitoring to track model performance degradation and input feature drift over time. Which monitoring profile type should they use?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Custom profile - Create a hybrid monitoring approach combining snapshot and time series profiles to capture both baseline comparisons and temporal trends."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Inference profile - Monitor the inference table to track model performance metrics, prediction drift, and input feature drift across time windows."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Time series profile - Monitor the inference table using timestamp-based windows to track model performance and input drift over time."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Snapshot profile - Monitor the complete inference table to compare current model performance against the baseline established at deployment."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The inference profile is designed specifically to monitor production model behavior using inference logs. It tracks model performance metrics, prediction distributions, and input feature drift across time windows, enabling detection of performance degradation and demographic data drift after deployment.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":169,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer wants to monitor the quality and stability of their machine learning model's predictions over time. They have a Delta table, retail_inference_log, which records each model prediction along with input features, a timestamp, and (when available) the true label. They need to detect data drift and monitor model performance trends using Databricks Lakehouse Monitoring, ensuring that alerts are triggered if the distribution of predictions or input features changes significantly. Which approach will set up monitoring for this use case?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create a monitor with the Inference profile on the retail_inference_log table, specifying the timestamp column and the columns for model inputs, predictions, and labels. Configure the monitor to compute drift and performance metrics over time windows."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create a monitor with the Inference profile on the retail_inference_log table, and specify a recent batch of production data as the baseline table for drift detection. Use this recent production data to compare against new data for drift and performance monitoring."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create a monitor with the Snapshot profile on the retail_inference_log table, so that metrics are calculated over the entire table each time the monitor runs and therefore is able to compare new values with previous ones to compute data drift."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create a monitor with the Time Series profile on the retail_inference_log table, specifying the timestamp column and including model input, prediction columns and the true label column. This will track drift in features and predictions over time, and model performance could also be tracked using a custom metric."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The Inference profile is specifically designed for monitoring production inference logs. By configuring it on the inference table with the timestamp, input feature columns, prediction column, and label column, Databricks Lakehouse Monitoring can automatically compute prediction drift, input feature drift, and model performance metrics over rolling time windows, and trigger alerts when significant distribution changes or performance degradation are detected.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":170,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is building a predictive maintenance model for a fleet of vehicles. They have two tables in their feature store: 1. A sensor_readings feature table with IoT data (e.g., engine_temp, oil_pressure) streamed continuously. This is a time-series table with vehicle_id as a primary key and ts as a timestamp key. 2. A maintenance_logs ground truth table that records when a vehicle component failed. This table includes vehicle_id and the exact failure_ts timestamp. The goal is to create a training dataset by joining sensor_readings to maintenance_logs to train a model that predicts failures. They want to join the feature data with the ground truth data to ensure point-in-time correctness and prevent data leakage during model training. Which approach will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Perform an inner join on vehicle_id and select the sensor reading with the timestamp that is closest to the failure_ts, whether it occurred before or after the failure."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Join the tables on vehicle_id and then manually filter to only include sensor readings that were recorded in the hour immediately preceding the failure_ts."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Perform an \"AS OF\" join, using the failure_ts from the maintenance logs to look up the latest sensor reading values that were recorded at or before that specific timestamp."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"For each failure, calculate the average of all sensor readings for that vehicle on the day of the failure and join that to the maintenance_logs table."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"An AS OF join ensures point-in-time correctness by retrieving the most recent sensor readings that occurred at or before the failure timestamp for each vehicle. This guarantees that only information available prior to the failure event is used for training, preventing data leakage and aligning with best practices for time-series feature joins in predictive maintenance models.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":171,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer needs to digitize millions of historical documents spanning 200+ years with vastly different handwriting styles, fonts, languages, document conditions, and paper types. To do this, the engineer wants to train thousands of specialized OCR models to extract text. The number of documents vary significantly. They need an efficient approach for this parallel model training task. Which approach suits their needs?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use Scikit-learn's ML library within a Pandas UDF to parallelize training across data partitions. Use groupby.applyInPandas."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use Ray map_in_batches to allocate resources based on the various sizes of models and data volumes."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use Spark MLlib with custom Spark UDFs to distribute across partitions."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use Spark for data preprocessing and orchestrate parallel model training jobs through Databricks workflows."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Ray's map_in_batches API is designed for large-scale, heterogeneous parallel workloads where tasks vary significantly in data size, model complexity, and resource requirements. It enables dynamic resource allocation and efficient scheduling across many specialized OCR training jobs, making it well suited for training thousands of models with uneven document volumes and computational needs.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":172,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is training a model to predict whether a customer will purchase a new product. They are experimenting with many algorithm types (e.g., Linear Regression, Logistic Regression, Decision Trees) and hundreds of hyperparameter combinations. They are using MLflow to track their runs, but managing and distinguishing between runs has become difficult. They need an MLflow technique that will organize and trace these experiments and that will follow commonly-accepted design patterns. Which technique will fulfill these requirements?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Log runs for each algorithm type to different MLflow Tracking servers to ensure the runs do not get mixed up."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use MLflows Automatic Logging feature to reduce the number of explicit log statements within each run to increase code readability."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use AutoML to automatically test a variety of different algorithms and models."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Set each algorithm type as a parent run and use nested runs for different hyperparameter settings within that parent run."}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Using parent runs for each algorithm and nested runs for the associated hyperparameter experiments follows a widely accepted MLflow experiment design pattern. This structure makes it easy to organize, compare, and trace large numbers of related runs, improving experiment management and interpretability when exploring many models and hyperparameter configurations.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":173,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has a real-time fraud detection model deployed that approves or blocks millions of transactions daily. They need to deploy a new version of the model with improved detection accuracy to this high-traffic, business-critical application. Because any model downtime could result in lost revenue or customer dissatisfaction, the engineer must ensure zero downtime and minimal disruption for end users. Leadership also requires that any rollback to the previous version be immediate if issues are detected with the new model in production. Which deployment strategy meets these requirements?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use a canary deployment by initially routing a small percentage of user traffic to the new model version, monitoring results, and gradually increasing exposure until all traffic uses the new version if no problems are detected."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace the current production model with the new version during a scheduled maintenance window, notify affected users of potential brief disruptions, and prepare to reroute requests to a backup if failures occur after deployment."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use a blue-green deployment for the new model, maintaining two separate production environments (one \"blue,\" one \"green\") and switching user traffic to the new version only after confirming it is healthy, enabling instant rollback if needed."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Deploy the new model in parallel with the old version, monitor both for a set period, and then notify all stakeholders to manually switch over to the new version at a coordinated time."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A blue-green deployment maintains two fully operational production environments and shifts traffic between them instantly. This approach provides zero downtime during deployment and allows immediate rollback to the previous model version if issues arise, which is critical for high-traffic, business-critical real-time systems.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":174,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Why is Delta Lake time travel useful in ML pipelines?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Faster model inference"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Reproducible training datasets"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Smaller datasets"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model tuning"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Time travel allows training on exact historical datasets.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":175,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"How can you save a trained Spark ML PipelineModel?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"pipeline.save()"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"pipelineModel.write().save()"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"pipeline.persist()"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"pipeline.store()"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Example: pipelineModel.write().overwrite().save(\"/model\")","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":176,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which MLflow function logs files like plots or datasets?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_param()"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_artifact()"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.log_metric()"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"mlflow.save_model()"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Artifacts include: charts model files datasets.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":177,"domain":"monitoring","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"What is commonly monitored in deployed ML models?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"GPU usage only"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Model drift and prediction quality"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Code style"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Notebook formatting"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Production ML systems monitor: data drift model drift prediction accuracy.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":178,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"Which approach is best for scoring large historical datasets?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Real-time REST API"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Batch inference with Spark"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Manual predictions"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Local Python script"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Spark allows distributed batch scoring across massive datasets.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":179,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer has deployed a fraud detection model in Databricks Model Serving to detect fraudulent transactions. The engineer wants to compare the model's predictions with the actual fraud classifications from the Fraud Ops team to monitor model performance. The Fraud Ops team uses a unique transaction_id to investigate fraudulent activity and persist their findings to a fraud_findings table. The engineer enabled inference tables on the endpoint, but they are not sure how to map the models' predictions to the Fraud Ops team's classifications. How can the engineer uniquely join the models' prediction to the fraud_findings table with the fewest code changes?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Store databricks_request_id returned from each model serving request and persist it to the fraud_findings table. Join the inference table with the fraud_findings table using databricks_request_id as the join key."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Join the inference table with the fraud_findings table using timestamp_ms as the join key."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Populate the client_request_id field with the transaction_id in the model serving request body. Join the inference table with the fraud_findings table using client_request_id (which contains the transaction_id) as the join key."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Modify the model to include an additional input: transaction_id. Log, register and deploy the new model. In the model serving request body, add transaction_id as an additional input feature. Join the inference table with the fraud_findings table using transaction_id as the join key."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Databricks Model Serving inference tables automatically log the client_request_id field for each request. By populating this field with the existing transaction_id in the request body, the engineer can directly and uniquely join inference predictions with the fraud_findings table using the same identifier, achieving accurate performance monitoring with minimal code changes and no model retraining or redeployment.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":180,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer needs to deploy a custom model using Databricks Model Serving. The model requires an external tokenizer file (for example, a vocabulary or pre-trained tokenizer) to function correctly. They need to ensure this tokenizer file is included with the model so it is available during model serving. How should they package this tokenizer file as part of the model deployment?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Include tokenizer file as part of the model's software dependencies by adding it to the conda_env specification."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the code_paths parameter in mlflow.pyfunc.log_model() to include the tokenizer file, assuming it's part of the custom Python logic needed by the model."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Access the tokenizer file directly from Volumes at model inference time, which requires pre-provisioning access to the Volume for every model serving endpoint."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the artifacts parameter in mlflow.pyfunc.log_model() to package the tokenizer file, referencing its path in Volumes."}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The artifacts parameter in mlflow.pyfunc.log_model is designed for packaging non-code assets required at inference time, such as tokenizer files. By logging the tokenizer as a model artifact and referencing its path, MLflow ensures the file is versioned with the model and automatically made available to Databricks Model Serving during inference.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":181,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is building an application that requires low latency data lookups in response to a user's question following a RAG based search. They want to ensure their users can receive as recent data as possible for urgent requests, so data should not be more than a few minutes late. The underlying data is a large table that may contain hundreds of gigabytes of data. Which data serving approach will suit their use case?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Online tables with snapshot sync mode"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"A fast database hosted in MLflow model serving"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Online tables with continuous sync mode"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Online tables with triggered mode and a time series key"}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Online tables with continuous sync mode are designed for low-latency serving while keeping data fresh within minutes. Continuous sync incrementally propagates updates from the large underlying table to the online store, ensuring near-real-time availability for RAG-based lookups without requiring full refreshes, which is ideal for urgent, freshness-sensitive queries on large datasets.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":182,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist needs to perform inference on a continuously updated Delta table called sales_data using an MLflow-registered Spark ML pipeline model (catalog.prod.sales_forecaster). Predictions must be written to a Delta table forecast_results, which must be updated with low latency leveraging a cluster with three executors. They want to maximize the efficient use of their cluster when doing this. Which approach will suit their needs?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q182-1.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q182-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q182-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q182-4.jpg"}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"This approach uses a Spark Structured Streaming read from the continuously updated Delta table and applies an MLflow-registered Spark UDF for inference. The model execution is distributed across the three executors, enabling parallel, low-latency scoring as new data arrives. Writing the results with writeStream efficiently updates the forecast_results Delta table incrementally, maximizing cluster utilization and aligning with best practices for continuous, scalable batch-stream inference in Databricks.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":183,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer wants to implement MLOps. They currently have three environments: dev, stage, and prod. They have many private PyPI packages that they use to train their models, and they are concerned that their environments will not be consistent between dev, stage, and prod. The engineer needs to follow enterprise scaling and CI/CD best practices to ensure that their environments are consistent. Which approach will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Define an all purpose cluster with the private PyPI packages installed, and use the same cluster config across environments."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Define dependencies for each task in their job(s) running the ML pipeline using Databricks Asset Bundles or Terraform."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the same DBR for ML runtime across environments and %pip install any additional packages at the top of the notebook."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the same DBR across environments."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Defining dependencies at the job or task level using Databricks Asset Bundles or Terraform aligns with enterprise MLOps and CI/CD best practices. This approach makes environment configuration declarative, version-controlled, and reproducible across dev, stage, and prod, ensuring consistent use of private PyPI packages without relying on mutable clusters or manual installation steps.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":184,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is building a propensity model for an e-commerce start-up. The company maintains 7GB of historical data and receives about 5MB of new transaction data daily. The goal is to generate daily purchase predictions for all users by 7:00 AM each morning. As the start-up is in its early stages, the data scientist must prioritize a highly cost-efficient approach. Which approach should the Data Scientist take?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use a multi-node compute and leverage distributed frameworks like SparkML to train the model. The model can then be scheduled as a nightly batch job that runs on a multi-node compute."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use a single-node memory compute to build a model with libraries like scikit-learn. The model can then be scheduled as a nightly batch job that runs on a single-node compute."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use a single-node compute to build a model with libraries like scikit-learn. The model can then be deployed as an always-on REST API so that users can query the API to get the predictions whenever they want."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use a single-node compute to build a model with libraries like scikit-learn. The model can then be integrated into an always-on streaming pipeline, to ensure immediate processing of incoming data in order to meet the SLA."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"With only 7GB of historical data and a small daily increment (about 5MB), a single-node memory-optimized cluster can comfortably train and score using scikit-learn without the overhead and cost of distributed compute. Scheduling a nightly batch job is the most cost-efficient way to meet a fixed daily SLA (7:00 AM) because the compute can be started only for the job run and then terminated, avoiding the expense of always-on serving or streaming infrastructure.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":185,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist has created a sales forecasting model, named sales-forecasting. The model is deployed to a model serving endpoint with a schema. They need to invoke this model using Python and would prefer to use a SDK function to make the request. Which method suits these requirements?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Import the MLflow Deployments class and use the predict method and provide the endpoint and input parameters."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use the built in ai_query function and provide the endpoints and request parameters."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Import the MLflow Deployments class and use the ai_query method and provide the endpoints and request parameters."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Make an API request to the MODEL_VERSION_URI and provide the dataframe_split as the request parameter."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"The MLflow Deployments SDK provides a predict method specifically designed to invoke Databricks model serving endpoints from Python. This method abstracts the HTTP request details, allows specifying the endpoint name directly, and cleanly passes input parameters that conform to the model's serving schema, making it the preferred SDK-based approach.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":186,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist at a company with rapidly increasing sales has deployed a scikit-learn model in production, which is retrained weekly on a single-node cluster. During the most recent retraining, the job failed due to an out-of-memory error. Upon investigation, the Data Scientist discovered that the training data had increased to 700GB as a result of the company's expanding customer base. Which approach will reliably resolve this issue in the long term?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Refactor the code to use the DeepSpeed library to shard the scikit-learn model across multiple nodes, enabling distributed model training on large volumes of data"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Refactor the code to use MLlib across multiple nodes, enabling distributed model training on large volumes of data"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Wrap the current model training code as a Spark UDF and apply it to the Spark DataFrame to distribute model training across multiple nodes."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Increase the size of the current single-node cluster to handle the increase of data while minimizing the need for code refactoring"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Spark MLlib is designed for distributed model training on large-scale datasets and can natively handle hundreds of gigabytes of data across multiple nodes. Refactoring to MLlib enables the training workload to scale with data growth, avoids single-node memory limitations, and provides a reliable long-term solution as the company's data continues to expand.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":187,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is building a machine learning pipeline to classify raw text using a Logistic Regression model in Spark using Spark MLlib's Pipelines. This pipeline has three stages: the Tokenizer (to split the raw text in tokens), a HashingTF (to transform tokens into hashes) and the Logistic Regression itself (to perform the classification of texts). The Spark DataFrame with the training data is called trainingDF and the one with the test data is called testDF. In order to do this, they use the following incomplete piece of code:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q187-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Which option correctly states: (i) The complete command to run model training; (ii) The complete command to execute the prediction on test data; (iii) The object type of the model object returned by the model training command."}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q187-2.jpg"}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q187-3.jpg"}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q187-4.jpg"}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q187-5.jpg"}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"In Spark MLlib, a Pipeline is trained using the fit method, which applies all estimator stages to the training DataFrame and returns a PipelineModel. Predictions are generated by calling transform on the fitted PipelineModel, which applies the full pipeline (tokenization, feature hashing, and logistic regression) to the test DataFrame.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":188,"domain":"experimentation","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is training a complex gradient-boosted model for fraud detection. The model uses dynamic threshold tuning during training and generates custom visualizations of feature drift. To ensure reproducibility and collaboration, they need to programmatically track: - Custom metrics (e g., adjusted_f1 for threshold variations) - Hyperparameters from nested configuration files - Drift visualization plots as PDFs Which approach implements this tracking in MLflow?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Enable mlflow.autolog() at the beginning of the code and rely on it to automatically detect and log custom metrics, parameters, and visualization files."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Store hyperparameters in a YAML file, save metrics to a CSV, and use mlflow.log_artifact() to upload both to the tracking server as artifacts."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Write all custom metrics and parameters to stdout during training and configure MLflow's tracking server to scrape these outputs."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use mlflow.log_metric(\"adjusted_f1\", value),mlflow.log_params(nested_config), and mlflow.log_artifact(\"drift_plot.pdf\") within an active MLflow run context, while enabling mlflow.autolog() to capture model-specific metadata automatically."}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Explicitly using mlflow.log_metric, mlflow.log_params, and mlflow.log_artifact within an active MLflow run provides precise, programmatic control over tracking custom metrics, complex hyperparameter configurations, and generated artifacts such as PDF visualizations. Enabling mlflow.autolog alongside this captures standard model metadata automatically while still allowing full flexibility for advanced, custom tracking needs.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":189,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer is implementing integration tests for an ML pipeline in Databricks. The current integration test runs the complete workflow but takes four hours to execute due to large dataset processing and extensive model training. They need to select an approach that will be the most effective for optimizing integration test execution while maintaining test reliability. The approach should also be based on MLOps best practices. Which approach will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Run integration tests only in the production environment using full datasets to ensure complete accuracy of the testing process."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Use small subsets of production-like data and reduce training iterations while maintaining the same pipeline structure and validation checkpoints in a staging environment that closely matches production."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Skip the model training phase entirely and only test data transformations and API endpoints using mock model predictions."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Replace integration tests with unit tests for each pipeline component to reduce execution time and focus on individual component validation in a staging environment that closely matches production."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Using smaller, production-like datasets and reduced training iterations preserves the full pipeline structure while significantly reducing execution time. This aligns with MLOps best practices by maintaining high-fidelity integration testing in a staging environment that mirrors production behavior, without the cost and delay of running full-scale training workloads.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":190,"domain":"lifecycle","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer wants to deploy a new change to their existing model training pipeline for a social media app. Currently, they use the Databricks Feature Store to create a training set to train their LightGBM model, which is then used in a Pandas UDF for batch inference to suggest potential friends to users. They have updated the feature engineering pipeline to include an additional feature function, which now computes the number of mutual friends a user has. Which test should they add to quickly inform them if something has broken in the test environment?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Implement an integration test that runs the entire pipeline from feature engineering to model inference in the production environment."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Add a unit test that verifies the new mutual friends feature function correctly calculates expected values on a fake dataset."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Add a unit test that verifies the new mutual friends feature function correctly calculates expected values on live data."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Implement an integration test that runs the feature engineering pipeline in the production environment."}]}],"answer":["B"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A unit test targeting the new feature function provides the fastest and most reliable signal that the recent change behaves correctly. By validating the mutual friends calculation on a controlled, fake dataset in the test environment, the engineer can quickly detect logic errors without the cost, risk, or latency of running full pipeline or production-based tests.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":191,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Data Scientist is building a product recommendation model that suggests additional items based on a customer's current shopping basket for an online merchant. The model needs to access the real-time basket contents at inference time to avoid recommending items already in the basket. The model will be deployed using Databricks Model Serving. The data scientist now wants to implement real-time feature engineering to incorporate the current basket contents into the model's prediction. Which approach will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Create a custom pyfunc model that processes the basket contents as part of the model's predict() method."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Write basket data to a feature table and publish to the online store for low-latency access."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Store basket data in a Delta table and configure automatic feature lookup during model serving."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Implement feature preprocessing in a separate microservice that feeds the model serving endpoint."}]}],"answer":["A"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Real-time basket contents are request-specific and only available at inference time, making them unsuitable for precomputed feature tables or online stores. Implementing a custom pyfunc model allows the predict() method to directly consume and process the basket contents included in the request payload, enabling real-time feature engineering while keeping the logic tightly coupled with model inference in Databricks Model Serving.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":192,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A retail company wants to better forecast their sales of each SKU in every store in order to more accurately distribute their products. To achieve this, a Data Scientist proposes scaling their existing forecasting model to forecast individually for each combination of SKU and Store ID. They have a cluster with 12 executors available in order to execute this. The current model is written using Pandas and the Prophet library for forecasting, with a Python function that receives a Pandas Data Frame with historic sales data as a parameter to train the forecasting model. For their next iteration, they want to improve the efficiency of this approach while using the least amount of effort and leveraging all available resources. Which approach will do this?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"1. Rewrite the function using the Generalized Linear Regression models available in Spark MLlib, which allows parameterizing to reflect a similar behavior to that of Prophet; 2. Perform a GROUP BY on the Spark DataFrame with the training data to group by Store and SKU; 3. Apply the Spark UDF to the grouped DataFrame with the apply function."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"1. Use a Spark query to identify all combinations of SKU and Store ID; 2. Run a loop through every combination calling the existing Python function using Spark repartition(12) function so it distributes across all available executors."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"1. Decorate the existing training function with the @pandas_udf; 2. On the Spark DataFrame with all training data perform a GROUP BY by Store and SKU; 3. Apply the decorated function to the grouped DataFrame with the apply function."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"1. Use Spark repartition(12) to scale across all executors; 2. Run to Pandas to convert the repartitioned DataFrame to Pandas so it can run on the original existing function; 3. Add the Store ID and SKU to the Prophet model as exogenous variables."}]}],"answer":["C"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"A grouped Pandas UDF lets you keep the existing Prophet-based pandas training function while distributing execution across the cluster. Grouping the Spark DataFrame by SKU and Store ID and applying the function runs one model per group in parallel on the executors, improving efficiency and leveraging all available resources with minimal refactoring.","multi":false,"equiv":[]},{"id":193,"domain":"deployment","stem":[{"t":"text","v":"A Machine Learning Engineer developed a dynamic pricing model in MLflow that requires values from the company's cloud database to generate predictions. At inference time the PyFunc model uses the cloud provider's Python SDK to retrieve the latest values from the database. The inference code works well in a notebook, but when the engineer deploys the model to Databricks Model Serving, they receive 401 errors saying the user is not authenticated when trying to access the database. The engineer deploys the code via the REST API with the following payload:"},{"t":"img","src":"assets/fig/q193-1.jpg"},{"t":"text","v":"Their Databricks administrators store cloud credentials under a Databricks secret scope called \"cloud_creds\" with key \"db_key\". These credentials can be used to authenticate to the cloud provider's SDK. Which change can the engineer make so the endpoint can authenticate to the remote database while avoiding storing the access tokens in plain text?"}],"choices":[{"key":"A","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Retrieve the secret values using a notebook with dbutils.secrets.get(scope=\"cloud_creds\", key=\"db_key\"). Log the model to MLflow with the access token value as a custom MLflow artifact. Register the model in Unity Catalog. Change the PyFunc model code to read the artifact and pass the secret to the cloud provider's SDK."}]},{"key":"B","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Retrieve the secret values using a notebook with dbutils.secrets.get(scope=\"cloud_creds\", key=\"db_key\"). Add \"environment_vars\": {\"DB_ACCESS_TOKEN\": \"\"} to the served_entity. Change the PyFunc model code to read the environment variable and pass the secret to the cloud provider's SDK."}]},{"key":"C","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Log the model to MLflow and store {{secrets/cloud_creds/db_key}} as a MLflow secret in /.mlflow/credentials. Register the model in Unity Catalog. When deployed in Databricks Model Serving, MLflow will automatically authenticate when the model server starts."}]},{"key":"D","blocks":[{"t":"text","v":"Add \"environment_vars\": {\"DB_ACCESS_TOKEN\": \"{{secrets/cloud_creds/db_key}}\"} to the served_entity. Change the PyFunc model code to read the environment variable and pass the secret to the cloud provider's SDK."}]}],"answer":["D"],"booklet":[],"note":"","explanation":"Databricks Model Serving supports secure secret injection by referencing Databricks secret scopes directly in the served entity configuration. By mapping the secret to an environment variable using the {{secrets/scope/key}} syntax, the model can securely access the credential at runtime without exposing it in plain text. The PyFunc model can then read the environment variable and authenticate to the cloud provider's SDK, resolving the 401 error while following security best practices.","multi":false,"equiv":[]}];
